\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Rise For Second Straight Session\b0\par\par\uc2 3,423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 21:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL STOCK INDEXES: \par DJ Indus     16340.88  +93.66 +0.58% \par Nasdaq        4331.58  +51.63 +1.21% \par NYSE Comp    10441.60  +64.58 +0.62% \par S&P 500       1872.60  +13.77 +0.74% \par Russell 2000  1204.15  +15.92 +1.34% \par TSM          19782.04 +154.65 +0.79% \par S&P/TSX      14371.56 +139.67 +0.98% \par FTSE 100      6605.28  +36.93 +0.56% \par FTSE 250     16354.20  +56.82 +0.35% \par Xetra DAX     9242.55  +61.66 +0.67% \par CAC40         4313.26  +41.30 +0.97% \par Nikkei Stock 14411.27 +133.60 +0.94% \par Hang Seng    21583.50 +109.55 +0.51% \par S&P/ASX 200   5344.60  +27.00 +0.51% \par NZSX-50       5135.66  +47.64 +0.94% \par Global Stock Indexes at 1925 GMT. \par  \par INTERBANK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATES: \par          Latest   Previous  % Chg \par                   Close \par USD/JPY 101.41-44 101.74-77 -0.33 \par EUR/USD 1.3929-32 1.3920-24 +0.06 \par GBP/USD 1.6588-92 1.6634-40 -0.28 \par USD/CHF 0.8733-36 0.8730-34 +0.03 \par USD/CAD 1.1137-40 1.1052-56 +0.77 \par AUD/USD 0.9127-30 0.9085-89 +0.46 \par NZD/USD 0.8624-28 0.8563-67 +0.71 \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates at 1950 GMT. \par  \par TREASURYS: \par 2Y   99 26/32 +1/32 0.351% -1.1BP \par 5Y   99 25/32 +4/32 1.546% -2.6BP \par 10Y 100 20/32 +5/32 2.679% -1.8BP \par  \par US STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rallied for a second-straight session Tuesday, bringing the S&P 500 within a fraction of another record high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average climbed 102 points, or 0.6%, to 16349. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pfizer\f28 \uc2 \f2  paced gains in 26 of the Dow's 30 components.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 index advanced 13 points, or 0.7%, to 1872 and the Nasdaq Composite Index gained 50 points, or 1.2%, to 4330.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The technology, health care and energy sectors led the advance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 has gained 1.7% in two sessions, and is just 0.3% away from its March 7 record closing high of 1878.04, which was the sixth record close of the year. Meanwhile, the Dow has gained 283 points, or 1.8%, this week, to trade 1.4% below its Dec. 31 record close 16576.77.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders say there was no single piece of news driving the market higher. They say concerns over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Ukraine remain in the background, but investors believe over the long term, it will end up as a nonevent. If recent upbeat economic data were providing the boost, they would expect a rise in bond yields, but yields are down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And with volumes light, they aren't seeing a big increase in buying, even by those who may have been selling as the market fell last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jim Russell, senior equity strategist at U.S. Bank Wealth Management, which oversees about $115 billion, said he has seem some flows into domestic equity mutual funds. Those flows may not be heavy, but given the light volumes and a lack of selling, it may be having an "outsize impact on the market," Mr. Russell said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Tuesday, Russian President Vladimir Putin signed a treaty formally annexing Ukraine's Crimea region after Crimeans voted overwhelmingly over the weekend to join Russia, as expected. But fears of escalating tensions were soothed after Mr. Putin said that while Crimea must be under Russian sovereignty, "we don't want a partition of Ukraine," and would defend Russia's interests by "diplomatic and legal means."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the economic front, housing starts for February slipped 0.2% to a seasonally adjusted annualized rate of 907,000, just above expectations of 905,000. Building permits grew 7.7%, exceeding forecasts of a 1.1% increase. The consumer-price index for February rose 0.1% on the month, and by 0.1% if food and energy are excluded, with both measures matching expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hertz Global Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 0.2%, erasing earlier losses, as the company's plan to spin off its equipment-rental business overshadowed fourth-quarter earnings and revenue that missed expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apple advanced 0.9% after the company updated its low-price iPad offering, adding a handful of new capabilities but keeping the price at $399.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dow component \f28 \uc2 \f2 American Express\f28 \uc2 \f2  tacked on 0.4% after saying late Monday it will sell half its corporate-travel business for $900 million to a group of investors including Certares and funds managed by BlackRock and Qatar Holdings. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell against the ruble after a further advance by Russia into Ukraine seemed unlikely, while the Australian dollar strengthened after its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suggested rates would remain steady for some time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian President Vladimir Putin on Tuesday signed a treaty to annex Ukraine's Crimea region, where voters had chosen to rejoin Russia in a Sunday referendum, but indicated there were no plans for a further push into Ukraine. The West has objected to the annexation and has imposed sanctions on senior officials.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell to 36.2344 rubles from 36.2865 rubles late Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sanctions imposed by the U.S. and \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , including asset freezes and travel bans on officials from Russia and Ukraine, were "relatively weak," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Sue Trinh in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the Australian dollar rose to 91.27 U.S. cents from 90.89 U.S. cents. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  released minutes of its March meeting, which showed expectations for interest rates to remain steady for a while.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns about the pace of growth in China, which is Australia's largest trading partner, had led to expectations that the RBA could cut rates further, said Chris Tevere, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com. "The most recent employment data out of Australia...coupled with the RBA's minutes have led to the realization that maybe the rate cuts that were priced in should be priced out," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. data released Tuesday showed a dip in housing starts in February, a rise in building permits to the highest rate since October, and a 0.1% increase in consumer prices last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE dollar index, a gauge of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength, rose to 79.38 from 79.363. The WSJ Dollar Index, an alternate measure that pits the dollar against more rivals, edged down to 73.01 from 73.07.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are looking ahead to Wednesday's conclusion of the first Federal Reserve meeting led by Chairwoman Janet Yellen. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's guidance on the future path of interest rates should be in focus, with some expecting a reduced emphasis on the unemployment rate. The Fed is widely expected to continue reducing its monetary stimulus at the meeting and will issue new economic projections.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was at $1.3931 from late Monday's $1.3927. The shared \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier Tuesday fell below $1.39 after a survey on German sentiment missed expectations. The ZEW indicator of economic sentiment dropped 9.1 points to 46.6, lower than expectations of a 52 reading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback fell against the Japanese yen, buying Y101.42 versus Y101.71 late Monday. The British pound was at $1.6591 from $1.6593. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US TREASURYS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury prices traded slightly higher on Tuesday as investors awaited the Federal Reserve's monetary-policy decision.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices eased from overnight highs after Russian President Vladimir Putin said Moscow has no intentions to partition Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark 10-year note added 5/32 in price to yield 2.679%, according to Tradeweb. The 30-year bond was up 2/32 in price to yield 3.625%. Bond prices rise when yields fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Open Market Committee kicked off its two-day meeting Tuesday, the first one under Chairwoman Janet Yellen. On Wednesday, the Fed will announce its policy decision and Ms. Yellen will hold a news conference.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market is put in a range now as they are waiting for the decision Yellen will make at the FOMC," said Millan L. Mulraine, deputy head of U.S. research and strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "This is a new Fed with a new slate of players. As an investor, you wouldn't want necessarily to prejudge that until you have more clarity on where Yellen wants to take the Fed."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed is expected to announce another $10 billion cut to its bond-buying program and officially step away from the 6.5% jobless threshold for raising the federal funds target rate. The decision could add pressure on short-term yields by pushing the market's expectation for the first rate increase further out. Therefore, the Treasury yield curve will steepen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors said there is a possibility that Ms. Yellen takes a more pro-growth approach at the meeting, for example, by reducing the pace of tapering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Just looking from the domestic perspective, I wouldn't rule that out," said Robert Tipp, chief investment strategist at Prudential Fixed Income, referring to the recent softness in economic data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Russia, Mr. Putin signed a treaty formally annexing the breakaway Ukrainian region of Crimea, but sought to reassure Ukrainians by signaling that Moscow isn't planning to send its troops further into Ukraine. The latest move by Mr. Putin eased some fears and sapped demand for safe-haven Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the economic front, U.S. consumer prices rose 0.1% in February from a month earlier, the Labor Department said Tuesday. But on a year-over-year basis, prices were up 1.1% from a year earlier, the weakest 12-month gain since October and far below the Fed's 2% target for annual inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We expected subdued data, but this is more subdued than we expected," said Kathy Jones, a fixed-income strategist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Schwab\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It is telling us there is still a long way to reach the 2% target." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks extended their rally into a second day Tuesday, with investors interpreting a speech by Russian President Vladimir Putin as a step back from further confrontation with Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Putin signed a treaty to annex the breakaway Ukrainian region of Crimea, but otherwise sounded a more conciliatory tone than many investors had expected. He said Russia isn't seeking "a partition of Ukraine" and would defend the interests of Russian speakers in the country by "diplomatic and legal means."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European shares, which had fallen as investors awaited the speech, rallied sharply, and were followed higher by U.S. markets. The Stoxx Europe 600 index closed 0.6% higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Russia, the RTS index climbed 4.2%, while the ruble bounced back from steep losses earlier in the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 18 Mar 2014 16:00 EDT \par Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Rise For -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Putin's speech didn't indicate he is looking for an escalation of the crisis. All the focus was on Crimea rather than any further areas of Ukraine," said Guillaume Tresca, emerging-market strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cr\u233\'00\'E9dit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With Mr. Putin largely steering clear of inflammatory rhetoric in response to the sanctions, markets are likely to turn their immediate focus to other issues, including the U.S. Federal Reserve meeting, Mr. Tresca said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even so, investors remain nervous.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't really see this as a de-escalation. The situation is still a very tense one," said Fran\u231\'00\'E7ois Savary, who helps oversee roughly $10 billion of assets as chief investment officer at Swiss bank Reyl.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Savary thinks European stocks have the potential to rise further as the economy improves, but isn't buying while the Ukraine situation remains so uncertain. Reyl reduced its exposure to stocks last week, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All major European indexes picked up from early losses, with Germany's DAX up 0.7% and London's FTSE 100 gaining 0.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among individual stocks, shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Intesa Sanpaolo\f28 \uc2 \f2  SpA rose 3.2% in Milan, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  was up 1.8% in Paris and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Santander SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 1.3% in Madrid.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Metro AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  picked up 3.8% after the German retailer said it has pushed back plans for an initial public offering of its Russian Cash & Carry business due to the political uncertainty in Russia and Ukraine. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stocks rose Tuesday, with Japan supported by a softer yen, while declining Chinese property companies were the focus in Hong Kong and Shanghai.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The region took its lead from the U.S., where stocks finished higher on Monday, after Wall Street shrugged off a referendum in Ukraine's Crimea region showing that the vast majority of voters wanted to secede and join Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The conflict is far from over. But markets don't regard the situation as critical," said Kengo Suzuki, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Mizuho Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The improved sentiment was most evident in Japan. Early weakness in the yen had a knock-on effect on Japanese stocks, with the Nikkei up 0.9% at 14411.27--putting the market on a recovery track from its 6.2% slump last week, its worst weekly performance since June last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In China, the Shanghai Composite rose less than 0.1% to 2025.20, as declines in developers weighed on the market. Data showed that gains in property prices in 70 Chinese cities continued to moderate in February after slowing in January for the first time in a year. News that a property developer in a small city in eastern China was unable to pay almost $400 million in debt also weighed on the sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Vanke Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . fell 2.1% in Shenzhen, while China Overseas Land & Investment fell 1.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index however gained 0.5% to 21583.50 due to a strong showing by one of its largest constituents. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tencent Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumped 5.2% after the internet giant cashed out of its investment in ChinaVision Media for a total of 1 billion Hong Kong dollars ($129 million).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Kospi was up 0.7% at 1940.21, and Singapore's Straits Times Index was up 0.05% at 3093.84. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The NZX-50 closed 0.9%, or 47.6 points, higher at 5135.66 as the market benefited from global strength, with risk sentiment improving during the U.S. trading session, Craig Investment Partners broker Belinda Stanley said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Asian and Australian markets have been really strong and we've really been dragged along by them," she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Xero rallied 3.7% as investors positioned themselves ahead of the stock's inclusion in the S&P/ASX's all ordinaries index. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN & NZ CORPORATE & ECONOMIC CALENDAR: \par LOCAL TIME/GMT \par 1045/2145  NZ   Q4 Balance of Payments \par 1045/2145  NZ   Q4 International Investment Position \par 1030/2330  AUS  Service Stream Ltd (SSMZF) EGM re share issue & non-renounceable rights issue \par 1030/2330  AUS  Jan Westpac-Melbourne Institute Indexes of Economic \par                 Activity \par 1100/0000  AUS  Mar Vacancy Report \par N/A        AUS  \f28 \uc2 \f2 David Jones Ltd (DJS.AU) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Interim 2014 Results \par  \par LONDON METAL EXCHANGE: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nickel closed at its highest price in almost a year on the London Metal Exchange Tuesday, after moving into a bull market amid persistent concerns over supply of the metal to the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The LME's three-month nickel contract was up 2% at $16,190 a metric ton at the PM kerb close, its highest settlement since April 11 last year. The metal hit an intraday peak at $16,235 a ton, representing a gain of more than 20% since its Jan. 9 low of $13,334 a ton, the definition of a bull market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nickel prices have been driven by restricted supply from Indonesia, which has banned exports of ore. Concerns that Crimea-related sanctions on Russia could disrupt exports of its metal have also propped up prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the U.S. and Europe have so far imposed sanctions only on individuals, "players on the nickel market still appear to assume that the Crimean crisis will escalate and that further-reaching sanctions will be imposed," analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nickel's break above $16,000 a ton Tuesday sparked technically driven buying, as stop-loss orders--automatic orders to buy at a certain level--were triggered, Standard Bank analyst Leon Westgate said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Nickel's break above $16,000 today should therefore see prices run higher; however, prices have moved a long way and it may need further fresh buying interest to give it a necessary shove," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME three-month copper closed 0.04% higher at $6,482 a ton Tuesday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US COMMODITIES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures strengthened Tuesday after a pipeline company said it would double the capacity of a pipeline connecting Oklahoma storage with Gulf Coast refineries sooner than previously expected. Light, sweet crude for April delivery settled up $1.62, or 1.7%, at $99.70 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Brent crude on the ICE futures exchange rose 55 cents, or 0.5%, to $106.79 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold and other precious-metals prices fell sharply after Russia's president said he doesn't want a partition of Ukraine, easing worries that Russia may move further into the country after its annexation of Crimea. Gold for April delivery, the most active contract, fell $13.90, or just over 1%, to settle at $1,359 a troy ounce on the Comex division of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Silver dropped nearly 2% to $20.862.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wheat futures jumped as the condition of the hard-red winter wheat crop in the U.S. declined amid dry, windy weather. Wheat futures for May delivery gained 18 cents, or 2.7%, to $6.92 1/2 a bushel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cotton prices climbed to a seven-month high as traders continued to worry that robust demand for the U.S.-grown fiber would lead to tighter domestic stocks. Cotton for delivery in May on the ICE Futures U.S. exchange ended 1% higher Tuesday at 92.93 cents a pound, the highest since Aug. 16.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Raw sugar for May delivery ended up 0.5% at 17.14 cents a pound. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES OF THE DAY: \par PUTIN SIGNS TREATY TO ANNEX CRIMEA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian President Vladimir Putin moved to annex the breakaway Ukrainian region of Crimea but sought to reassure Ukrainians by saying Moscow has no further designs on its southern neighbour's territories. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NYSE SAID TO BE FRONT-RUNNER FOR ALIBABA LISTING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The New York Stock Exchange is the front-runner to win the high-profile listing for the shares of Chinese e-commerce giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alibaba Group Holding Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., according to people familiar with the matter. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. HOUSING STARTS FALL SLIGHTLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unseasonably cold weather appears to have chilled new-home construction in February, but underlying figures suggest building could be poised for a spring rebound. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. CONSUMER PRICES REMAIN NEARLY FLAT IN FEBRUARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The consumer-price index increased at a seasonally adjusted 0.1% in February from a month earlier, a sign that inflationary pressures remain tame in the economy. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GM CEO APOLOGIZES FOR RECALL DELAY, VOWS CHANGES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Motors CEO Mary Barra intensified her campaign to show how the auto maker has changed, saying a newly appointed global safety chief will meet with her once a month to discuss issues. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GOOGLE\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 VIACOM\f28 \uc2 \f2  RESOLVE YOUTUBE COPYRIGHT LAWSUIT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Viacom\f28 \uc2 \f2  said they have resolved copyright litigation between the two companies. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Viacom\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in a $1 billion lawsuit, had claimed \f28 \uc2 \f2 YouTube\f28 \uc2 \f2  allowed users to post copyrighted content without permission. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par APPLE UPDATES LOW-PRICE IPAD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apple updated its low-price iPad model, adding a handful of new capabilities and a more powerful processor, but keeping the price at $399. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par UKRAINE CRISIS WEIGHS ON GERMANY'S OUTLOOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German economic expectations fell sharply in March as a result of the continued uncertainty regarding Russia and Ukraine, a key sentiment indicator showed. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EURO ZONE REPORTS TRADE SURPLUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 18 countries that share the euro had a surplus in their trade of goods with the rest of the world in January, an indication that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area's continued economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 18, 2014 16:00 ET (20:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140318ea3i0035e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
