\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: China Drives Yuan Lower by Largest Amount in 18 Months -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 990 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 12:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey, Alen Mattich and Colin Ng\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP: While the world is distracted by other events -- Russia and Ukraine sparring, the hunt for a missing airplane, the mysteries of bitcoin -- China has been quietly engineering a major realignment in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the world's most important exporter of merchandise.\par \par \uc2 The significance of this shift can't be underestimated for global trade. It's not that the 1.6% devaluation of the yuan over the past two months materially alter China's competitiveness -- although the fact that it is happening at a time when some emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have rebounded gives it more significance. It's that it means a markedly more aggressive level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Chinese authorities. The conventional wisdom is that they are -- ironically -- being more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  now so as to set up a less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in the future. They are thought to be bringing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a level they are comfortable with before allowing it to float more freely. Either way, the changes will affect the price paid for goods made in China--and that means all of us. (MC)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: The yuan slumped against the dollar Monday after the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  dropped the value of its daily fixing by the most in a year and a half, continuing a pattern of erratic movements that many observers think is a prelude to widening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band. This came after data over the weekend showed Chinese exports fell by 18.1% on-year in February, while inflation came in below expectations, adding fuel to claims that the world's second-largest economy is heading for a hard landing.\par \par \uc2 The data may have exacerbated the yuan's decline, but analysts noted that the trade figure likely reflected rampant over-invoicing a year ago as well as the effect of the Lunar New Year holiday that fell in early February this year. Mostly, then, this slippage for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as a deliberate attempt by the authorities to shift its trajectory and make way for an expected change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down the road. Meanwhile, benign inflation gives the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  room to ease interest rates later this year if the economy seems unlikely to reach the government's 7.5% growth target. (MA)\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE: Russian President Vladimir Putin formally endorsed a Crimean push for independence from Ukraine in a referendum set for later this month while U.S. President Barack Obama said he would meet with his Ukrainian counterpart in Washington on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Crimean impasse could still trigger a serious regional crisis, but for now it seems to have stabilized temporarily. With neither the U.S. nor the \uc2 European Union\uc2  looking to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  forcefully, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems set to get its way. (AM)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN: The government revised down its fourth-quarter GDP to 0.7% from the initial reading of 1%. Meanwhile, the country posted a current-account deficit of Y1.59 trillion ($15.4 billion) in January, its largest ever.\par \par \uc2 Weak capex and consumer spending were behind the GDP revision, which will likely intensify the debate about whether the government should go forward with a sales-tax hike in April as planned. For the current account, it was the fourth straight month of deficits, the longest spell on record, and reflects both tepid exports and a surge in import costs due to the weak yen and higher imported fuel volumes. (MA)\par \par \uc2 FRANCE: January industrial production fell 0.2% on the month against expectations of no change and following a 0.3% decline in December.\par \par \uc2 The French economy is struggling for growth. The January industrial-production data are particularly worrisome. Forecasters had expected it to level off following December's decline, but, instead, it fell further. The \uc2 French \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to look for a 0.2% GDP expansion during the first quarter, but with this sort of data coming through, and disappointing business surveys -- sentiment in the services sector has also been glum -- it's hard to see where the growth is coming from. (AM)\par \par \uc2 ITALY: January Industrial production rose 1.0% on the month and 1.4% on the year against expectations of a 0.4% monthly rise and 1.1% annual fall.\par \par \uc2 Italian industry started the year strongly. Undoubtedly, demand for Italy's high end manufactured goods is lending support -- the rich seem to be the world's only robust consumers. Emerging market wobbles, however, raise questions over how long this boom might be sustained. (AM)\par \par \uc2 COMING UP:\par \par \uc2 CANADA: 8:15 a.m. EDT. February Housing Starts. [Expected 190,000 annualized, up from 180,248 in January.]\par \par \uc2 A pickup in February would help spur keep construction jobs in check against the impact of a slowdown in what had been a booming Canadian housing market. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 12:40 p.m. EDT \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2  President \uc2 Charles Evans\uc2  speaks at Columbus State University.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Evans is considered a dove, clearly in favor of a measured approach to tapering the bond-buying program. He is not currently a voter on the FOMC. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.K.: 8:01 p.m. EDT (12:01 a.m. Tuesday, London) February BRC same store sales [From up 3.9% year on year in January]\par \par \uc2 The U.K. economy is booming, not least thanks to strong consumer demand. But with real earnings still contracting and credit growth still relatively subdued, one or both will need to pick up for consumption growth to be sustained. (AM) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 10, 2014 07:07 ET (11:07 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140310ea3a0015p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
