\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End Mostly Higher; Jobs Data Support\b0\par\par\uc2 3,080 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 21:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL STOCK INDEXES: \par DJ Indus     16452.72  +30.83 +0.19% \par Nasdaq        4336.22  -15.90 -0.37% \par NYSE Comp    10511.91  -13.61 -0.13% \par S&P 500       1878.04   +1.01 +0.05% \par Russell 2000  1203.32   -1.23 -0.10% \par TSM          19839.26   +4.56 +0.02% \par S&P/TSX      14299.08  +27.16 +0.19% \par FTSE 100      6712.67  -75.82 -1.12% \par FTSE 250     16559.45 -112.30 -0.67% \par Xetra DAX     9350.75 -192.12 -2.01% \par CAC40         4366.42  -50.62 -1.15% \par Nikkei Stock 15274.07 +139.32 +0.92% \par Hang Seng    22660.49  -42.48 -0.19% \par S&P/ASX 200   5462.30  +16.40 +0.30% \par NZSX-50       5125.65  +10.86 +0.21% \par Global Stock Indexes at 1925 GMT. \par  \par INTERBANK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATES: \par          Latest   Previous  % Chg \par                   Close \par USD/JPY 103.24-28 103.24-28  0.00 \par EUR/USD 1.3867-82 1.3867-82 +0.00 \par GBP/USD 1.6711-16 1.6711-16  0.00 \par USD/CHF 0.8775-86 0.8775-86  0.00 \par USD/CAD 1.1083-88 1.1083-88  0.00 \par AUD/USD 0.9065-76 0.9065-76  0.00 \par NZD/USD 0.8464-72 0.8464-72 +0.00 \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates at 1950 GMT. \par  \par TREASURYS: \par 2Y  99 24/32  -2/32 0.373% +2.3BP \par 5Y  99 11/32 -10/32 1.636% +6.9BP \par 10Y 99 21/32 -14/32 2.790% +5.3BP \par  \par US STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks closed mostly higher Friday after data showed more jobs were created in February than economists expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest employment report showed that the economic recovery, while still relatively slow, remained on track. It supported investors' belief that recent disappointing economic data was a temporary stumble because of cold winter weather, but it didn't give them enough of a reason to make further big moves into stocks. Traders also expect the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to continue to scale back its economic-stimulus measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 30.83 points, or 0.2%, to end at 16452.72. It had been up as many as 84 points earlier in the session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 rose 1.01 points, or 0.1%, to 1878.04, closing at a record high but off an intraday peak of 1883.57. The S&P 500 posted a second-straight weekly gain, and has climbed 7.7% since it closed at a 3 1/2-month low on Feb. 3.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nasdaq Composite Index fell 15.90 points, or 0.4%, to 4336.22.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nonfarm payrolls increased by 175,000 in February, exceeding expectations of 152,000. Meanwhile, the unemployment rate ticked up to 6.7% from January's 6.6%, versus expectations of a decline to 6.5%. January payroll growth was revised up to 129,000 from an original estimate of 113,000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The better-than-expected jobs data came on the eve of the bull market's five-year anniversary. The S&P 500 has nearly tripled since bottoming out at 676.53 on March 9, 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders attributed the midday swoon that pulled the market off its highs to investors cashing in on some gains in light volume ahead of the weekend, given the uncertainty surrounding Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 John Stoltzfus, chief market strategist at Oppenheimer Asset Management, which oversees about $86 billion, said the jobs report is "highly supportive" of the way the market has rallied over the last month in the face of disappointing data, but it is reasonable to "take some money off the table ahead of the weekend."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is comfortable to be investing in stocks now, but it is never safe," Mr. Stoltzfus said. "That said, it is a market in which the overall trend outweighs other considerations."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although fears over the situation in Ukraine have faded over the last week, it was still a source of some concern for investors. Russia's upper house of parliament pledged Friday to support Crimea if it seeks to join the Russian Federation, should its people vote to become part of Russia in a referendum on the issue scheduled for March 16.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Big Lots\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumped 22% after the closeout retailer reported fiscal fourth-quarter earnings that were within the range of its forecast, while revenue exceeded analyst estimates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Public Service Enterprise Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 0.4% after the power company unveiled a $12 billion five-year capital spending plan in an effort to boost earnings growth for its utility business.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coupons.com soared on its debut as a public company, with the stock trading 92% above its initial public offering price of $16. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar strengthened against most major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday after the February U.S. jobs report came in above expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Labor Department reported that the U.S. economy added 175,000 jobs in February, above expectations of 152,000. The unemployment rate ticked higher to 6.7% from 6.6% in January, as more people joined the workforce.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's strong jobs report reversed the trend of soft employment and other disappointing economic data over the past few of months. It suggested the Federal Reserve will likely continue to reduce its monthly bond purchases, which have weighed on the value of the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This isn't necessarily a sign that the economy is beginning to strengthen, but it is a confirmation that the recent weakness we've seen is more related to the weather and less indicative of a broader economic slowdown," said Brian Daingerfield, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at RBS Securities in Stamford, Conn. "The follow-through of this jobs report will be continued strength in the dollar."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rose to a six-week high against the yen, at Y103.52, before settling at Y103.29, up 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback pared losses against the euro, trading at $1.3870, from $1.3891 before the data, down 0.1%. Earlier Friday, the euro had surpassed $1.39 for the first time since Oct. 31, 2011, rising to as high as $1.3915. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US TREASURYS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury bond prices fell Friday and capped the biggest weekly selloff in three months as an upbeat reading of the U.S. labor market sapped demand for haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sellers piled in after the Labor Department showed a bigger-than-forecast 175,000 non-farm jobs added last month, sending the 10-year yield to a six-week high. When bond prices fall, their yields rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year note was 14/32 lower in price, yielding 2.790%, according to Tradeweb.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year note's yield rose by about 0.13 percentage points for the week. The yield, a key benchmark for setting borrowing costs for consumers and businesses in the global arena, remains at historic lows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The U.S. economy has underlined strength despite the adverse weather in January and February" and the Fed will continue to scale back monetary stimulus, said Gary Pollack, who helps oversee $12 billion in assets as head of fixed-income trading in New York at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank AG\f28 \uc2 \f2 's private wealth management unit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, some investors and analysts don't expect the yield to reach 3% any time soon, a level it last traded in early January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Factors keeping the rise in check include signs of uneven growth in the U.S. and abroad, ongoing geopolitical concerns in Ukraine and the Fed's pledge to keep short-term interest rates near zero until the economy shows a more robust pace of recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The rise in bond yields in 2014 is not likely to be as disorderly as it was during the turbulent period last summer mainly because investors are more prepared now for rates to rise, having altered their fixed-income allocations accordingly," said Tony Crescenzi, senior market strategist and portfolio manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pacific Investment Management Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in Newport Beach, California, which has $1.91 trillion in assets under management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thomas Roth, executive director in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond trading group at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities USA Inc. in New York, said there have been buyers taking advantage of Friday's selling in bonds which sent the 10-year yield up to 2.821%, the highest level since Jan. 23.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year yield around 2.8% is still attractive to some investors, because "we still have a lot of work do to get the economy back on track," he said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock markets slid into the close Friday, as investors sold off ahead of the weekend on fears the crisis in Ukraine could escalate. Indexes had earlier pared losses after the highly anticipated U.S. nonfarm-payrolls report beat expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Stoxx Europe 600 index slumped 1.3% to close at 333.06, losing 1.5% on the week and marking the first weekly slide since January. Germany's DAX declined 2.0%, while France's CAC-40 was 1.2% lower. The U.K.'s FTSE 100 shed 1.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Leading the decliners Friday, shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Getinge AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  tumbled 21% after the medical-technology company said its profit trend for the first quarter will be weak due to a lower level of invoicing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fugro NV\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 2.1% after the oil-field-services firm posted 2013 earnings that missed expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banks were lower across most of Europe, with shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 2.7% in Frankfurt, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  off 2.8% in Paris and heavyweight \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  1% lower in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the opposite direction, shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air France-KLM SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumped 4.4% after the airline said passenger traffic rose 1.4% in February, boosted by a rise in both long and medium-haul flights.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More broadly, most European stock markets closed in the red, ending a week that was marked by increased tensions between Russia and Ukraine. The crisis intensified Friday afternoon after part state-owned \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it might halt gas deliveries to Ukraine as it did in 2009, because the country hasn't paid for its February supply. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gazprom\f28 \uc2 \f2  also said Ukraine owes it $1.89 billion for gas, according to The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  has condemned Russia's occupation of Ukraine's Crimea region and earlier in the week unveiled a set of sanctions to punish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . France's foreign minister, Laurent Fabius, reportedly said Friday that a second round of sanctions could be on the cards if the first round doesn't work.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chatter that big U.S. pension funds were rebalancing their asset portfolios by dumping equities and instead moving into bonds, also appeared to weigh on the European stock markets. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian markets rose Friday at the end of a positive week for the region, ahead of a monthly labor report in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 Mar 2014 16:15 EDT \par Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen softened to its lowest level against the dollar since January, as the greenback strengthened after jobless benefit claims fell last week to a three-month low, raising expectations for a strong labor report later in the global day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The best Asian performer for the week was Japan, where the Nikkei continued its volatile trading as stocks in Tokyo made outsize movements to small moves in the yen. The Nikkei was up 2.9% for the week and added 0.9% on Friday, closing at 15274.07.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In China, stocks were little moved, with shares in energy companies offsetting falls in property firms. The Shanghai Composite was down 0.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Petroleum & Chemical Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . jumped 2.5% in Shanghai and 4.6% in Hong Kong on expectations the energy sector will benefit from reforms in state-owned enterprises. \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroChina\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 1.4% on the mainland. Gree Real Estate fell 3.8% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Merchants Property Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 0.7%, as profit-taking hit developers. In the month-to-date, China Merchants has risen 6.8%, even after the day's declines. Gree has been on a sharp downward trend since it hit an almost four-year high in mid-February. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The NZX-50 closed up 0.2%, or 10.86 points, at 5125.65 as the index continues to hit new record highs on the back of a solid reporting season.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Restaurant Brands rose a further 0.7% after the company said Thursday that total sales for the fourth quarter were up 6.4%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 0.8% likely on profit-taking after the stock's share price rose 8.6% after a strong result the previous week. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN & NZ CORPORATE & ECONOMIC CALENDAR: \par LOCAL TIME/GMT \par 1045/2145  NZ   4Q Economic Survey of Manufacturing \par 1000/0200  AUS  Northern Star Resources Ltd General Meeting re approval of issue of shares \par N/A        AUS  Energy World Corporation Ltd (EWC.AU) Interim 2013 Results \par                 (EWC.AU) \par N/A        AUS  CSL Limited (CSL.AU) Trading statement \par  \par LONDON METAL EXCHANGE: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper futures closed sharply lower on the London Metal Exchange Friday, after crashing to seven-and-a-half-month lows amid renewed concerns over Chinese economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The LME's flagship three-month copper contract was down 3.8% at $6,781 a metric ton at the PM kerb close, its lowest price since July 31.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese solar-equipment maker Shanghai Chaori Solar Energy Science & Technology Co. became the first Chinese company to default on a bond traded in the mainland Friday, rattling investor confidence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Metals were rocked by the Chinese corporate default," said Michael Turek, director of metals trading with futures brokerage Newedge. "This underscores not only the systemic structural debt issue there but also the Chinese central government's apparent willingness to forgo short-term GDP growth to skim speculative excesses for longer-term benefit."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While copper's deep descent could attract some opportunistic buying at lower levels "it will take some really positive Chinese news to lift copper back from the doldrums," said Chris Beauchamp a market analyst at IG. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US COMMODITIES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. oil futures ended Friday with a small rally, after a volatile week driven by geopolitical disruptions in Ukraine and the appearance of bearish fundamental factors in domestic supply data. Prices for West Texas Intermediate crude rose $1.02, or 1%, to settle at $102.58 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The global Brent contract finished up 90 cents, or 0.8%, at $109.00.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold and silver futures dropped as investors came to grips with the prospect of continued stimulus reduction by the Federal Reserve in the wake of better-than-expected U.S. employment data. Gold for April delivery, the most active contract, fell $13.60, or 1%, to settle at $1,338.20 a troy ounce on the Comex division of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Silver for May delivery fell 64.6 cents, or 3%, to settle at $20.928 an ounce.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wheat futures rose to a three-month high on speculation that drought in the U.S. southern Great Plains will curb production in the U.S., the world's biggest exporter of the grain. Wheat futures for March delivery gained 4 3/4 cents, or 0.7%, to $6.46 1/4 a bushel, and May contracts, the most-active futures, jumped 8 cents, or 1.2%, to $6.54 a bushel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cotton futures whipsawed in intraday trade, rising to a six-month high of 93.35 cents a pound before running out of steam and falling back to end down 0.4% at 91.27 cents a pound.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders took profits after sugar settled at consecutive four-month highs. Raw sugar for delivery in May settled at 18.01 cents a pound, down 1.7% on the day. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par WEEKEND HIGHLIGHTS: \par Vietnam Finds Suspected Fragments of Missing Jetliner in Water \par Ukraine Hopes for Diplomatic Solution: Prime Minister \par China February Exports Slide \par China February PPI Down 2.0% on Year \par China February CPI Up 2.0% on Year \par China Online Funds Pressure Deposit Ceiling, Ex-PBOC Vice Governor \par Ono Shareholders Move Ahead With Listing Plans \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2  Dreamliner Makes Emergency Landing in Honolulu \par FRIDAY'S HIGHLIGHTS: \par U.S. NONFARM PAYROLLS UP 175K; JOBLESS RATE 6.7% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. nonfarm payrolls increased by a seasonally adjusted 175,000 in February, and the unemployment rate ticked up to 6.7%. Economists expected payrolls would rise 152,000 and the unemployment rate would fall to 6.5%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. PROBES \f28 \uc2 \f2 SOCI\u201\'00\'C9T\u201\'00\'C9 G\u201\'00\'C9N\u201\'00\'C9RALE\f28 \uc2 \f2 , CR\u201\'00\'C9DIT AGRICOLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French banks \f28 \uc2 \f2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cr\u233\'00\'E9dit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  are under U.S. investigation for alleged money laundering and sanction breaches involving Iran, Cuba and Sudan, according to a person with direct knowledge of the matter. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOEING REVEALS CRACKS IN  DREAMLINER WINGS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2  revealed that a manufacturing problem had caused hairline cracks in the wings on some of its 787 Dreamliner jets, requiring inspections on 42 aircraft and delaying the delivery of some to airlines. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. TRADE GAP WIDENS SLIGHTLY IN JANUARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. trade deficit widened 0.3% to $39.1 billion in January from a month earlier. Exports climbed 0.6% to $192.5 billion. Imports rose 0.6% to $231.6 billion. Economists forecast a deficit of $38.4 billion. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RUSSIAN PARLIAMENT WOULD BACK CRIMEA SECESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's upper house of parliament will support Crimea if it seeks to join the Russian Federation, the speaker of the upper house of parliament, Valentina Matvienko, said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par J.P. MORGAN WHISTLEBLOWER GETS $64 MILLION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Former \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2  employee Keith Edwards--who provided tips leading to a payment of $614 million by the bank to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  over insurance on home loans--will get $63.9 million for his efforts. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORMER JEFFERIES TRADER FOUND GUILTY OF FRAUD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A jury found former \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jefferies Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  trader Jesse Litvak guilty of violating federal securities laws by misrepresenting bond prices to his clients in an effort to boost his own trading profits. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RANBAXY RECALLS GENERIC LIPITOR IN U.S. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ranbaxy Laboratories\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the India-based generic-drug maker beset by manufacturing-quality problems, has issued another recall of generic versions of the cholesterol drug Lipitor in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 09, 2014 16:15 ET (20:15 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140309ea39000e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
