\par\par \b \uc2 Russian assets marked down for Moscow's actions in Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 08:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, March 7 (Reuters) - Russian assets declined across markets on Friday, marked down compared to other emerging markets for Russian actions in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the rouble was down 0.4 percent against the dollar at 36.28 after losing 0.4 percent in the previous session, while other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Turkish lira and the South African rand saw gains.\par \par \uc2 Investors were somewhat reassured by the \uc2 European Union\uc2  taking only minor steps in condemning Russian actions in Ukraine, but unnerved by the Crimean parliament's vote in favour of annexing the peninsula to Russia and a decision to bring forward a referendum on the issue.\par \par \uc2 "While for now, the sanctions mean only visa restrictions (by the U.S.), the decisive position of the Crimean authorities could destabilise the situation, because the volume of accusations against Russia can increase," Oleg Dushin, an analyst at the Zerich investment house, said in a morning note.\par \par \uc2 The rouble was on a path to end the week more than 1 percent weaker against the dollar. It was trading 0.4 percent down on the day against the euro at 50.30.\par \par \uc2 This has left the rouble 0.4 percent weaker at 42.53 against the dollar-euro basket that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses as a guide for the rouble's nominal exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The rouble remains the "underdog in the emerging markets space", analysts at VTB Capital wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which since Monday has increased its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "As political risks remain elevated, we believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be reluctant to unwind its emergency actions (including higher \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  volumes and higher rates) until the risk premium in \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related assets diminishes," VTB Capital analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Both Moscow indexes, the rouble-traded MICEX index and the dollar-denominated RTS, traded 0.1 percent lower, at 1,264.1 and 1,337.2 points respectively, underperforming the broader MSCIEF index, which was up 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 "It's all very sad, the broader emerging markets index MSCI Emerging Markets is near the maximums this year and our MICEX on Monday tested four-year minimums," Zerich's analysts wrote.\par \par \uc2 Absent any further dramatic developments in Ukraine, analysts expected equities to trade largely flat in Moscow, and the rouble to remain under pressure.\par \par \uc2 For rouble poll data see\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see\par \par \uc2 Russia in graphics: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/dun63s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/dun63s}}}\uc2  (Reporting by Lidia Kelly; Editing by Kevin Liffey)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-10T07:05:27.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140307ea37007ka\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
