\par\par \b \uc2 India Morning Call-Global Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 03:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 7 (Reuters) - EQUITIES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK - U.S. stocks mostly rose on Thursday, with the S&P 500 closing at yet another record on better-than-expected jobless claims data and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to keep rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 But the overall sentiment was cautious ahead of Friday's all-important U.S. nonfarm payrolls report and tensions between Ukraine and Russia.\par \par \uc2 The CBOE Volatility Index or VIX, Wall Street's so-called fear gauge, ended up 2.3 percent at 14.21. The VIX generally moves inversely to the performance of the S&P 500 and is often used to hedge against a market decline.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 LONDON - Britain's top share index edged higher on Thursday, buoyed by updates from as Aviva and \uc2 Aggreko\uc2  , although lack of action by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and uncertainty over Ukraine capped gains.\par \par \uc2 British equities tracked euro zone stocks lower, after ECB President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  offered no new measures to boost lending in the region. Speculation that he would had boosted European stocks in early trade.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 TOKYO - Japan's Nikkei share average rose to a new five-week high on Friday morning as a weak yen lifted risk appetite following Wall Street's gains on better-than-expected U.S. jobless claims and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep its rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei gained 1.1 percent to 15,307.78, the highest since Jan. 29 after rising 1.6 percent on the previous day. For the week, the index has added 3.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG - Hang Seng Index set to open up 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY - The euro hovered near a two-month high against the dollar early on Friday following a relief rally when the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its interest rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Caution ahead of the closely-watched U.S. nonfarm payrolls later in the day was seen capping further gains.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 TREASURIES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK - \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  debt prices fell on Thursday as fears over a war in Ukraine abated, helping drive benchmark yields to their highest levels in a week, and as traders prepared for Friday's key non-farm payrolls report.\par \par \uc2 Markets await the results from a referendum vote due in 10 days that will decide whether Crimea will become a part of Russia.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 COMMODITIES\par \par \uc2 GOLD\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE - Gold held on to sharp overnight gains on Friday to trade near a four-month high and looked poised to log its fifth straight week of gains as a weaker dollar and geopolitical tensions in Ukraine lifted its safe-haven status.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 BASE METALS\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY - Nickel held near nine-month highs on Friday and is on track for its biggest weekly gain since mid-January with supply tightened by Indonesia's ban on ore exports, while copper steadied on concerns over demand from top consumer China.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 OIL\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK - U.S. oil rose on Thursday after falling to near three-week lows as traders reconsidered the geopolitical risk posed by worsening diplomatic relations over \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Crimea.\par \par \uc2 The American benchmark fell by more than $1 about two hours before the settlement, then reversed losses to end the day higher after comments from U.S. President Barack Obama indicated the crisis between Ukraine and Russia, one of the world's biggest oil producers, was not easing as much as traders thought.\par \par \uc2 For a full report, double click on (Compiled by Indulal PM)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-10T02:58:24.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140307ea37002xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
