\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares gain, yen stays on defensive as Ukraine fears ease\b0\par\par\uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * European stocks seen dipping following wide swings\par \par \uc2 * Yen's gains continue to unravel after Putin suggests conflict not imminent\par \par \uc2 * Asian shares gain, Tokyo's Nikkei rises 1.2 percent\par \par \uc2 * Focus shifts to Thursday's ECB meeting, Friday's U.S. jobs report\par \par \uc2 By Shinichi Saoshiro and Lisa Twaronite\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 5 (Reuters) - Asian stocks gained and the safe-haven yen remained on the defensive after a sharp tumble on Wednesday, following remarks from Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that allayed fears of an imminent military conflict in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Putin said Russia reserved the right to use all options to protect compatriots who were living in "terror" in Ukraine, but that force was not needed for now.\par \par \uc2 European stocks were seen dipping on Wednesday following the wild swings of previous sessions.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, financial spreadbetter IG expected Britain's FTSE 100 to open down 11 points, or 0.2 percent, Germany's DAX to open 27 points lower, or 0.3 percent, and France's CAC 40 to open down 13 points, or 0.3 percent lower.\par \par \uc2 "While the Ukraine saga looks set to rumble on and Europe's markets get set for a slightly negative open, the focus can now shift back to the mundane matters of economic data," Michael Hewson, chief market analyst at \uc2 CMC\uc2  Markets, said in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 With wariness over Ukraine on a lower boil, focus shifted back to fundamentals, notably Thursday's \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy meeting and Friday's U.S. nonfarm payrolls report.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Wednesday, Tokyo's Nikkei climbed 1.2 percent, buoyed after Putin's remarks helped the S&P 500 attain another record closing high on Tuesday. MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan rose 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Soichiro Monji, chief strategist at \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2  in Tokyo, said the markets took Putin's words positively though the Russian leader did not rule out military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "While it looks like \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's control of Crimea is becoming a fait accompli, there is no further escalation and no major sanction by the G7 other than skipping the G8 meeting," Monji said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the widespread relief, market watchers warned of further jolts from the crisis in Crimea.\par \par \uc2 "The easing of geopolitical tensions saw a reversal of yesterday's movements in most asset markets. However, tensions remain high and suggest some further volatility in financial markets while the situation in Ukraine remains uncertain," said Janu Chan, an economist at St. George Bank in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar, already on a bullish footing on revived risk appetite, received a further boost after data showed Australia's economic growth had beaten forecasts.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie was at $0.8958 from a low near 89 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 Australia's major trading partner China said on Wednesday it would maintain its economic growth target for 2014 at around 7.5 percent, as expected, and push forward convertibility of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the statement was an indication that China would widen the yuan's trading band going forward, further signalling a possible end to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's one-way appreciation\par \par \uc2 The dollar index traded at 80.178, moving away from Friday's two-month low of 79.688.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters expect Friday's U.S. nonfarm payrolls report for February could show a more solid increase of 150,000 jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 The yen remained on the back foot after a heavy reversal on Tuesday. The dollar was buying 102.14 yen, moving away from a one-month low of 101.20 hit on Monday, while the euro bought 140.23 yen, after touching a two-week low of 138.75 yen on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The euro was nearly flat on the day against the greenback at $1.3728, below Friday's high of $1.3825.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to tread water ahead of Thursday's ECB monetary policy meeting. The ECB could take steps to bolster the region's recovery, as euro zone inflation has been running well below the ECB's target of just under 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 On the commodities front, U.S. crude was up 14 cents at $103.47, after falling $1.59 on Tuesday. The contract hit its highest level since Sept. 20 on Monday at $105.22.\par \par \uc2 Three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange rose 0.4 percent to $7,078 a tonne, after gains of 1.2 percent in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was nearly flat at $1,336.55 an ounce after dropping 1.2 percent on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-5T08:00:13.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140304ea3400zw0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
