\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Russian stocks fall back as Ukraine weighs, rouble buoyed by cbank comments\b0\par\par\uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 16:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Russian stocks dip as Ukraine talks continue\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine crisis expected to weigh on stocks in March\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says rouble undervalued, cites strong trade data\par \par \uc2 By Jason Bush and Zlata Garasyuta\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, March 5 (Reuters) - Russian shares retreated on Wednesday, halting the previous day's rebound, as diplomatic efforts to ease the crisis in Ukraine showed little sign of providing a breakthrough.\par \par \uc2 The rouble nevertheless strengthened after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it regarded the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as undervalued.\par \par \uc2 Shares fell 2 percent early in the day, recovered in the afternoon but slipped back slightly in late trade.\par \par \uc2 At 1445 GMT the rouble-denominated MICEX index was down 0.4 percent at 1,351 points and the dollar-denominated RTS was 0.2 percent lower at 1,182 points.\par \par \uc2 After plummeting by 10-12 percent on Monday on nervousness after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Crimea region of Ukraine, the stock indexes had rebounded by 5-6 percent on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Probusinessbank trader Evgeny Volkov, however, described the rebound as "purely technical".\par \par \uc2 "Unfortunately the market will probably try to return to the levels reached on Monday. The rhetoric of the U.S. remains hostile and foreign capital is leaving," he said.\par \par \uc2 Investors are reducing their exposure to Russia due to uncertainty about how the situation in Ukraine will play out. The United States has warned it could impose sanctions on Russia, which could hit Russia's already slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 Diplomatic efforts to end the armed stand-off in Crimea continued on Wednesday, with Russian foreign minister Sergei Lavrov due to meet his U.S. counterpart John Kerry in Paris.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  said that Germany continued to view the situation in Ukraine as "alarming" and "unacceptable".\par \par \uc2 "Players in the Russian market will most probably adopt a waiting position, but the dead-end situation around Ukraine will limit purchases," said Sergei Suvarov, head analyst at \uc2 Russian Standard\uc2 . "The market is very speculative and the ball is in the court of hedge funds."\par \par \uc2 Although concerns about Ukraine weighed on the market, it was supported by positive developments in other global markets, with Wall Street hitting new record highs on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "But let's look the truth in the face: our market won't be a good object for investments this month," Andrei Vernikov, analyst at Zerich Capital, said in a note.\par \par \uc2 He said the Russian market "will not soar" at least until March 30, when pro-Russian authorities in the Ukrainian region of Crimea have announced that they intend to hold a referendum on the region's status.\par \par \uc2 However, the rouble, which plunged to a record low against the dollar on Monday, forcing heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up, strengthened for a second day on Wednesday and was boosted by bullish comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nabiullina said she saw no fundamental reasons for the weakening of the rouble, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered it to be undervalued given Russia's rising current account and trade surpluses in the first two months of this year.\par \par \uc2 She also reiterated the bank's 5 percent inflation forecast by year-end, and its commitment to shift to an inflation- targeting policy regime, planned from next year.\par \par \uc2 At 1445 GMT the rouble was up 0.1 percent at 36.02 against the dollar, and was 0.2 percent stronger versus the euro at 49.45.\par \par \uc2 It was also up 0.2 percent against the dollar-euro basket , at 42.06.\par \par \uc2 "In the last three months the rouble in relation to the dollar has definitely lost practically all connection with fundamental factors," commented Investcafe analyst Timur Nigmatullin in a note.\par \par \uc2 The rouble has shed 9 percent against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it had spent the equivalent of $11.4 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves on Monday to support the rouble, stemming a sell-off caused by the escalation of the Ukraine crisis.\par \par \uc2 "It is too early to talk about a stabilisation of the situation, as the tone of statements from American and European officials isn't weakening," ING economist Dmitry Polevoy said.\par \par \uc2 "In these conditions the rouble could easily move (down) further, especially in advance of the referendum date in Crimea and in the absence of obvious ways out of the crisis."\par \par \uc2 For rouble poll data see\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see\par \par \uc2 Russia in graphics:\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-8T15:51:19.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140305ea3500ei9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
