\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Russian stocks, rouble rebound as Putin breaks silence on Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 894 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 15:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Russian stocks up more than 5 pct\par \par \uc2 * Sentiment lifted as Moscow orders troops back to base\par \par \uc2 * Putin slams "coup" but plays down invasion threat\par \par \uc2 * Investors wary of economic effect of Ukraine crisis\par \par \uc2 By Jason Bush and Zlata Garasyuta\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, March 4 (Reuters) - Russian assets rebounded on Tuesday as President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said he would only use force in neighbouring Ukraine as a last resort.\par \par \uc2 The mood on the markets was calmer after panic selling on Monday when escalating tensions between Ukraine and Russia sent Moscow's main stock indexes plummeting and forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to unexpectedly raise interest rates - by a hefty 150 basis points - and burn its way through as much as $12 billion of reserves to prop up the rouble.\par \par \uc2 By 1400 GMT on Tuesday the rouble-denominated MICEX share index had rebounded 5.2 percent to 1,356 points, while the dollar-denominated RTS index was up 6.1 percent at 1,184 points. The indexes have yet to fully recover Monday's more than 10 percent declines.\par \par \uc2 "From morning today we've seen good demand for liquid shares from foreign hedge-funds and also long-only funds. Oddly enough American investors are also buying," said Andrei Yarnikh, equity salesman at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It looks like Western countries aren't so intent on introducing sanctions against Russia, and the threat of bringing (Russian) troops into Ukraine has also lessened."\par \par \uc2 Markets were cheered initially when Putin ordered the end of large-scale military exercises in Russia, and gained a further boost after his comments at a press conference, his first on the crisis.\par \par \uc2 Putin slammed Western powers for backing what he called an "armed seizure of power" in Ukraine, but also emphasised that he saw no immediate need for Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which he described as a "last resort".\par \par \uc2 He also said Russia was willing to work with Western states and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  on financial assistance for Ukraine, and cooperate with the new authorities in Kiev although Russia does not regard them as legitimate.\par \par \uc2 "People are bored of expecting a bad scenario in Ukraine and have started to believe that there will be no military scenario and a confrontation of the Ukrainian army with Russia won't happen," said Oleg Dushin, senior analyst at Zerich Capital Management.\par \par \uc2 "The fear that Russian troops might go into, say, Kharkiv (in eastern Ukraine), isn't now on the agenda. You could say that the question of a Third World War has been removed," he said.\par \par \uc2 The rouble also rebounded, after sliding 2 percent on Monday. At 0930 GMT it was up 1.2 percent to 36.10 against the dollar and was up 0.8 percent at 49.75 versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 Against the dollar-euro basket it was up 1 percent at 42.20.\par \par \uc2 "Many in the market took Putin's speech as an attempt to reverse gear in the conflict, or tried to convince themselves of this," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a large Russian bank, who cautioned that "it isn't over yet".\par \par \uc2 COOLER HEADS\par \par \uc2 In a note to investors, Mattias Westman, founding partner of \uc2 Prosperity Capital Management\uc2 , a major portfolio investor in Russia, said "a series of bad decisions has been made by all sides in this situation" in Ukraine, but that cooler heads were now expected to prevail.\par \par \uc2 "The situation, in the last day or so, appears to have stopped escalating and different parties appear to be trying to find a peaceful solution," he said.\par \par \uc2 Investors, however, continued to be wary about the economic implications of Russia's involvement in the Ukraine crisis.\par \par \uc2 The United States said on Monday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  had violated international law with its military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine and that it would look at a series of economic and diplomatic sanctions to isolate Moscow.\par \par \uc2 The Russian economy was showing signs of slowing before the crisis, with manufacturing activity shrinking for a fourth month in a row in February.\par \par \uc2 A salesman at a Russian investment company said institutional investors still expected a wave of outflows from Russia and the region this week.\par \par \uc2 He said "the market is pricing a completely different valuation range for Russia as a consequence of recent events" because of the increased risk caused by Putin's unpredictability and economic difficulties that Russia may experience for an extended period.\par \par \uc2 "When the further development of events becomes more-or-less clear then it would be worth taking a look at murdered liquid Russian stocks," said Vladislav Silaev, trader at Alfa Capital.\par \par \uc2 Russia's finance ministry\uc2  announced on Tuesday it was suspending purchases of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the domestic market to replenish its Reserve Fund because of high volatility on financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The purchases, equivalent to 3.5 billion roubles ($95.7 million) a day over three months, had been one of the factors weighing on the rouble.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on Tuesday it had moved the rouble's floating corridor by an unusually large 35 kopecks to 35.75-42.75 against the basket the previous day.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the bank said it was increasing the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threshold to move the corridor by 5 kopecks to $1.5 billion from $350 million, and that it would revise its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  parameters on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-7T14:56:54.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140304ea34009vl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
