\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Gains on Yen as Ukraine Tensions Ease -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 18:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:19 EST - An easing of tensions in Ukraine has pushed the dollar up against the safe-haven yen as investors return to buying riskier assets. The move more than reverses the greenback's losses against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday. With risk appetite back up, USD/JPY's rise comes in line with rising US stocks and higher \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  yields. "Dollar-yen is following the S&P, which is rallying on the perceived developments between Ukraine and Russia," said Brad Bechtel, managing director at Faros Trading. "We're not out of the woods, but the market has reacted positively as a result." Pair up 0.7% at 102.16. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:51 EST - The Canadian dollar slumps in recent trading, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back into negative territory for the day against the US dollar. Some analysts speculate the loonie will become sensitive to a possible election being called in Quebec, which is currently governed by the separatist Parti Quebecois. But for now, Velocity Trade's Jasmin Valade attributes the moves to flow-driven trading ahead the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  policy announcement Wednesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to maintain its dovish stance. USD/CAD at 1.1111, up 0.31%, according to CQG. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com; @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 11:00 EST - Peru's inflation should begin a downward trend in coming months, following a higher-than-expected 0.6% jump in February, which brought the 12-month inflation number to 3.78%, significantly higher than the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2 's 1% to 3% target range. "We expect inflation to show a downward trend in the following months, as we anticipate that GDP will keep evolving around potential and international food prices will maintain a downward bias," \uc2 BBVA\uc2  Continental said. Brokerage Inteligo said that, "inflation should return to the levels of 2.8%-3.0% in the following months." (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:48 EST - HCA announcing a $3B debt refinancing came as the high-yield corporate-bond market had its slowest new-issue month in nearly 5 years, says Canaccord, putting issuance down 25% to start 2014. That as last week, "a lot of high-yield managers were at an industry conference and it was a busy earnings week for high-yield companies"--meaning those firms had to stay out of the market. "Over the next two weeks, we expect to see mostly small issuers in the market and continued low issues. We expect the volume and size of new issues to start picking up and recover pace mid-March." It all comes as The Wall Street Journal reported over the weekend that the SEC is looking into how banks divvy out corporate bonds. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:36 EST - After swinging back and forth between net-long and net-short positions since the start of the year, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's active Treasury clients came out dead even in the latest weekly survey. With the 23% pro-Treasury accounts offsetting the 23% anti-Treasury accounts, those neutral on the market remained dominant at 54%. Such a divided stance reflects how uncertain the near-term forecast is, since most still believe yields will rise later this year. Weather-impacted data threaten to pause Fed tapering, while concerns about Ukraine spur flights to safety, hurting the mountain of short bets stacked up against Treasurys when 2014 started. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 10:32 EST - Strong rebound rally in US stocks indicates investors see low/no risk from any Ukraine fallout, but Citi's analysts warn that events could trigger "a range of negative outcomes that could weigh upon markets." Firm says turmoil has exposed divisions between Russia, US and Europe and "re-activated numerous Cold War-era fault lines; and have highlighted a risk factor we have long felt is consistently underpriced by markets: the extent to which the transgression of geopolitical 'red lines' can trump economic and trade considerations." Citi also says "tensions between Russia and the US/EU are bound to sink further from their recent lows, with significant potential to generate trade and commercial disruption (including the threat of sanctions, as well as reducing cooperation on global threats such as Syria and Iran." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1513 GMT [Dow Jones] According to \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 's STEER model of fair value, USD/CAD should be trading around 1.0640. The undervaluation of CAD also corresponds to the bank's own \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  positioning analysis, which shows the Loonie is one of the few \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  where positioning remains at an extreme short level: within the G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's the most shorted. Taking both STEER and positioning signals together, the bank expects CAD weakness to be unwound over the coming week. USD/CAD now at 1.1080. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1508 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary can't just weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without limit, even if it wanted to, says CIB Bank in reaction to some market views that \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may want a much weaker HUF. An EUR/HUF rate of 330 to 350 would give rise to financial stability risks and increase Hungary's financing costs--because of its large foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt--to a degree which the country cannot afford, CIB economist Mariann Trippon says. Cutting the main monetary policy rate too low would also risk a weakening of the HUF too much, she adds. A slightly weaker HUF may boost Hungary's competitiveness, but most exporters feel happy with an EUR/HUF exchange rate of around 295, Ms. Trippon says. The HUF is already slightly undervalued based on the real effective exchange rate, she adds. CIB forecasts the year-end EUR/HUF rate at 295 as it expects interest-rate hikes from the \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 2H. Risk to that HUF forecast is to the upside and it's also a risk that lower US bond purchases may still lead to market jitters, Ms. Trippon adds. EUR/HUF is at 309.88 versus 312.77 at Tuesday's open. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:51 EST - Canada's French-speaking province Quebec looks headed toward an election this spring; reports Tuesday morning suggest April 7. The opposition Liberals seem to be beefing up their bench strength on the economy, with some heavyweight economists expected to declare their candidacy for the party in three districts today. They include \uc2 Carlos Leitao\uc2 , chief economist at Quebec's \uc2 Laurentian Bank\uc2 , \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  economist Martin Coiteux and Jacques Daoust, former president of Investissement-Quebec, an economic development agency in the province. It's not the first time economists have jumped into the fray of politics in Quebec: former National Bank chief economist Clement Gignac was elected in 2009 and served as a cabinet minister in the Liberal government of that time. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1447 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on Monday, selling more than $10 billion to ease pressure on the ruble, which tumbled to all-time lows. Normally, it spends a few hundred million dollars a day to prop up the ruble and was set to further limit its presence on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, vowing to let the ruble float freely in 2015 and instead focusing on inflation. "After yesterday's decision, the market has completely lost its trust in the CBR's guidance. We believe that even if the CBR presents yesterday's decision mix as an emergency measure, the market now understands that ruble flexibility will be allowed only within a limited level," \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2  says. It has revised the year-end exchange rate forecast for USD/RUB to 38 from 36. On Tuesday, the pair firmed 1.1% to 36.09 against the dollar.(andrey.ostroukh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:44 EST - Treasury prices lose nearly all of Monday's gains as Russia softens its stance on Ukraine, while the dollar is down against most emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on similar relief. While the political tension there is far from over, the rest of the week will have major US data to take investors' minds off Ukraine, namely Friday's nonfarm-payrolls report. The 10-year note is down 13/32 to yield 2.653% while the dollar falls 0.9% versus the Turkish lira to TRY2.210 and 1% against the South African rand at ZAR10.7906. The Russian ruble has bounced back too, the dollar off 1.2% at RUR36.09. Meanwhile, the yen being a for-now safe-haven is reversing, with the dollar rising 0.5% to Y101.96. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 8:07 EST - This month's investment letter from Bill Gross brings to mind tree rings, and central banks are the middle. "Change the price of credit at the center and you change the price of assets at the outer extremities." So, "if the center holds, if global central bankers can convince investors that their abnormal policies can recreate a semblance of the old normal economy, then risk assets at the outer edges" like equities and real estate "will have higher future returns than otherwise...As long as artificially low policy rates persist, then artificially high-priced risk assets are not necessarily mispriced." But the question has long been when will central banks stop distorting markets, letting credit prices normalize. For investors, Gross says, "Timing that exit is obviously difficult and perilous, but critical for surviving in a new epoch." (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 04, 2014 12:19 ET (17:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140304ea34002zg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
