\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's Strive To Prop Up Ruble Ruins  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Credibility -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 15:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1447 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily on Monday, selling more than $10 billion to ease pressure on the ruble, which tumbled to all-time lows. Normally, it spends a few hundred million dollars a day to prop up the ruble and was set to further limit its presence on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, vowing to let the ruble float freely in 2015 and instead focusing on inflation. "After yesterday's decision, the market has completely lost its trust in the CBR's guidance. We believe that even if the CBR presents yesterday's decision mix as an emergency measure, the market now understands that ruble flexibility will be allowed only within a limited level," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. It has revised the year-end exchange rate forecast for USD/RUB to 38 from 36. On Tuesday, the pair firmed 1.1% to 36.09 against the dollar.(andrey.ostroukh@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 9:44 EST - Treasury prices lose nearly all of Monday's gains as Russia softens its stance on Ukraine, while the dollar is down against most emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on similar relief. While the political tension there is far from over, the rest of the week will have major US data to take investors' minds off Ukraine, namely Friday's nonfarm-payrolls report. The 10-year note is down 13/32 to yield 2.653% while the dollar falls 0.9% versus the Turkish lira to TRY2.210 and 1% against the South African rand at ZAR10.7906. The Russian ruble has bounced back too, the dollar off 1.2% at RUR36.09. Meanwhile, the yen being a for-now safe-haven is reversing, with the dollar rising 0.5% to Y101.96. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 8:07 EST - This month's investment letter from Bill Gross brings to mind tree rings, and central banks are the middle. "Change the price of credit at the center and you change the price of assets at the outer extremities." So, "if the center holds, if global central bankers can convince investors that their abnormal policies can recreate a semblance of the old normal economy, then risk assets at the outer edges" like equities and real estate "will have higher future returns than otherwise...As long as artificially low policy rates persist, then artificially high-priced risk assets are not necessarily mispriced." But the question has long been when will central banks stop distorting markets, letting credit prices normalize. For investors, Gross says, "Timing that exit is obviously difficult and perilous, but critical for surviving in a new epoch." (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones] The hurried tapping of keyboards is audible in financial centres as analysts work out which countries are most exposed to the Ukraine and Russia - and then which countries are most exposed to those countries, and so on down the line. Capital Economics' keys have been pounded and it reckons the UK, at least is safe enough. Phew. Local trade and financial exposure to both Ukraine and Russia is relatively small, Capital says, and the biggest threat might stem from any major and sustained rise in commodity prices. So the UK's all-but criminal negligence when it comes to Emerging Market business has some upside after all... david.cottle@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1222 GMT [Dow Jones]- It turns out Putin sees "no need" to send troops into Ukraine. "This looks, feels and smells like a Putin climb-down, if there is such a thing," comments Tim Ash of Standard Bank. He says that Mr Putin was probably struck by the unified Western response and the threat of sanctions. In any case, Crimea is now occupied, albeit not annexed - but it's not at all clear how Ukraine plans to get it back, while the Russian presence will loom over the new Ukrainian administration, making life for the country's freshly-installed new leaders even more difficult. - (serena.ruffoni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1151 GMT [Dow Jones] The Russian President's speech Tuesday clearly questioned the legitimacy of interim government in Kiev even if it was reassuring to the extent that it said Russia won't be sending troops into Ukraine yet, says Citi's Emerging Market strategist Luis Costa. All in all Mr. Costa says it wasn't a hugely confrontational declaraion and, as such, has given an additional boost to EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/TRY is down by 1%, EUR/PLN by 0.7% and USD/ZAR by 0.6%, trading at 2.21, 4.1775 and 10.81 respectively. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1145 GMT [Dow Jones] The downtrend in EUR/CHF will remain on course for 1.2068/70 while resistance at 1.2176/87 caps the upside. But the pace of the sub-dividing downtrend from the Jan. 21 lower high at 1.2376 is likely to slow down, epitomized by this week's bounce from a 13-month low at 1.2101. The structure of the medium-term downtrend from 1.2376 will remain robust while projected resistance at 1.2176 holds, which has so far successfully capped EUR strength during the current session. And with former range lows at 1.2185/87 looming just above the market, it would require a concerted wave of bull pressure to question the more dominant downtrend. The body of congestion between 1.2118 and 1.2135 provides a defence area protecting the 1.2101 low. EUR/CHF is at 1.2167. -By Francis Bray, Dow Jones Chief Technical Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1144 GMT [Dow Jones] During the euro-zone crisis SEK appeared as an attractive alternative to EUR, backed up by Sweden's strong fundamentals and low and stable public debt. This time around however SEB says the crisis is about increased geopolitical uncertainty, and should the Ukrainian situation intensify further, the krona's defensive qualities from a few years back are unlikely to prevent it from weakening further as risk appetite falls. EUR/SEK now at 8.8590. gary.stride@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1123 GMT [Dow Jones] Core government bond prices slipped further - pushing yields higher - as Russian President Putin says that there is "no need yet" to use military force in Ukraine and that Russia is not considering annexation of the Crimea. March Bunds trade 0.44 lower at 144.70, June Treasurys lose 14/32 and trade at 124-175 while June gilts trade 0.42 lower at 109.66 with volume in all three front-month contracts now picking up. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1117 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is brave, not blind, CIB Bank says, referring to the popular Hungarian saying "this horse is not blind but brave," with regards to the \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to slashing interest rates as much as it just can. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's room to cut rates further, however, from the current all-time low of 2.70% is "very, very limited," CIB Bank economist Mariann Trippon says. Ms. Trippon expects Hungary's main rate to bottom at 2.50%-2.60% and forecasts a reverse in the rate policy course during 2H. Expects the main rate to end 2014 at 3.5% since CPI is slated to rise from January's 0% on the year due to a pickup in the economy. Inflation "is not a bad thing," it would help the government reduce public debt as a ratio of GDP, she adds. CIB expects CPI to increase to 3%, meeting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 3% inflation target, by year-end. Sees 2014 GDP growth at 1.5%, less than the government's 2% forecast. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1115 GMT [Dow Jones]--Italy's sovereign bonds are now correctly priced against their German equivalents and that means anybody staying with the long Italy/short Germany position "now faces a genuine risk," says GaveKal analyst Charles Gave. Notes that the position's strong return of past two years was similar to the broad euro-zone equities index, but on much lower volatility. Going forward, deflation appears inevitable, he says. Italy 10-year BTP yield is 3.46%, 190 basis points over German 10-year bunds.(chris.emsden@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1112 GMT [Dow Jones] The March Bund future has been marked lower during Tuesday's current session, and support at 144.66 is under threat. But backup support at 144.52 on Tuesday's Market Profile chart indicates limited scope for weakness during the current session. Single print support at 144.13 marks the pivotal level of last week's profile, and this would only be exposed on a break below 144.52. Recapturing ground above 145.08 is required to re-open Monday's contract high at 145.42, and the broader picture painted on the continuation chart suggests the all-time traded high at 147.20 in May 2013 is coming within reach. The March Bund future is at 144.70. Market Profile is an intraday charting technique, seeking to evaluate market value as it develops during the day. The technique displays price on a vertical axis, and time/activity on a horizontal axis, creating bell curves that show normal and variable distributions in financial markets. -By Francis Bray, Dow Jones Chief Technical Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 04, 2014 09:47 ET (14:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140304ea34002ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
