\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's Strive To Prop Up Ruble Ruins  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Credibility -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 194 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 15:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1447 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on Monday, selling more than $10 billion to ease pressure on the ruble, which tumbled to all-time lows. Normally, it spends a few hundred million dollars a day to prop up the ruble and was set to further limit its presence on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, vowing to let the ruble float freely in 2015 and instead focusing on inflation. "After yesterday's decision, the market has completely lost its trust in the CBR's guidance. We believe that even if the CBR presents yesterday's decision mix as an emergency measure, the market now understands that ruble flexibility will be allowed only within a limited level," \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2  says. It has revised the year-end exchange rate forecast for USD/RUB to 38 from 36. On Tuesday, the pair firmed 1.1% to 36.09 against the dollar.(andrey.ostroukh@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 04, 2014 09:47 ET (14:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140304ea34002bs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
