\par\par \b \uc2 Ukraine Crisis Pressures Russian Markets -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh \par \uc2 1,529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 02:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--As Russia's military secured the Crimean peninsula, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a record low and its stock market plunged in the face of U.S. and European warnings of sanctions over the incursion into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The Obama administration took the first steps late Monday, suspending military cooperation with Russia as well as talks aimed at boosting trade and investment, in a bid to isolate Moscow.\par \par \uc2 President Barack Obama said Russia is "on the wrong side of history" as well as international law. Further military moves into Ukraine, he warned, "will be a costly proposition for Russia."\par \par \uc2 The State Department said more punitive measures were likely if Moscow does not pull back, but wouldn't give details. Spokeswoman Jennifer Psaki indicated they would go beyond things like visa freezes to measures with economic bite.\par \par \uc2 European Union\uc2  foreign ministers backed off any immediate action on sanctions Monday, but warned the bloc was ready to act within days if Moscow didn't reverse course.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin has dismissed any sanctions as inappropriate and showed no sign of backing down under the pressure, which so far has been primarily in the form of tough talk.\par \par \uc2 The developments highlighted the West's limited options for deterring a Russia determined to reassert its influence in a neighbor with which it shares centuries of common history and culture as well as myriad economic ties.\par \par \uc2 Russian troops occupying the Crimean peninsula in southern Ukraine tightened their control, raising pressure on the few Ukrainian military units based there, but stopping short of violence. The Kremlin drew up plans for urgent financial aid to its new clients in the predominantly Russia-speaking region, and even ordered preparations for building a massive bridge to link it directly to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's prime minister admitted that his government has little near-term hope of regaining control of the region.\par \par \uc2 President Vladimir Putin on Monday visited massive military maneuvers under way outside St. Petersburg, arriving in a helicopter to view tanks and paratroopers in live-fire exercises.\par \par \uc2 The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply raised interest rates Monday to help stem the ruble's slide, which could push Russia's already sputtering economy into recession if the hike lasts for long.\par \par \uc2 But the impact would be eased thanks to high oil prices--they rose Monday on the turmoil--which would mean the Kremlin's coffers would likely remain flush with rubles needed to shore up public support. Russia is one of the world's biggest energy exporters.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Mario Draghi said the direct impact of the Ukraine crisis on the continent's economy would be relatively limited. Still, he added, "the geopolitical dimensions" of the crisis could have effects that "go beyond the actual links."\par \par \uc2 Some tremors were already being felt: U.S. wheat futures surged 4.6% as traders fretted that the escalating crisis will slow grain exports from Ukraine, a major producer.\par \par \uc2 Rising tensions are clouding sales forecasts and investment plans for global corporate giants such as \uc2 Renault SA\uc2  and \uc2 PepsiCo Inc\uc2 . that are exposed to Russia's market.\par \par \uc2 Shares in European banks with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Russia or Ukraine, including Italy's \uc2 UniCredit SpA\uc2  and France's \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale SA\uc2 , got clobbered as markets across Europe were rattled. The Stoxx Europe 600 index fell 2.3%, and countries with closer economic ties to Russia suffered more. Germany's benchmark DAX dropped 3.4%. Poland's main stock index fell 5.2%.\par \par \uc2 In Brussels, European foreign ministers condemned what the \uc2 European Union\uc2  foreign policy chief Catherine Ashton called Russia's "act of aggression" in Crimea.\par \par \uc2 The ministers said that "in the absence of de-escalating steps by Russia, " the EU would consider suspending talks with Russia on easing visa procedures and on a trade and economic agreement.\par \par \uc2 The warning was an attempt to strike a balance, refraining from hasty action that would foreclose Russia's ability to save face, while simultaneously signaling that the EU wouldn't hold off for long.\par \par \uc2 So far, the pressure on Moscow has been limited mainly to stern rhetoric and canceled meetings, including preparations for the \uc2 Group of Eight\uc2  summit in Sochi, Russia, in June. The White House said it won't send a presidential delegation to the Paralympic Games in Sochi either.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, at least, there appears to be little immediate appetite for broader sanctions that would upend trade with Russia.\par \par \uc2 France, for instance, is on schedule to deliver the first of two Mistral carriers, a deal valued at EUR1.4 billion ($1.9 billion), to the Russian Navy by the end of the year, according to \uc2 DCNS\uc2 , the French firm building the high-tech amphibious assault ships.\par \par \uc2 EU foreign ministers discussed a possible arms embargo on Russia on Monday, but rejected it for now.\par \par \uc2 Asked Monday whether Moscow's incursion was forcing Paris to rethink its trade ties, French Foreign Minister Laurent Fabius said: "We're not there. For the moment, we are trying to stop Russia's mobilization in Ukraine and establish a dialogue."\par \par \uc2 Diplomats said that Germany argued strongly in the meeting for Europe to move cautiously and to ensure that channels of communication were kept open to the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 "In this phase, we in Europe don't have to and must not take action based on rumors, imperfect information and news agency reports," said German Foreign Minister Frank-Walter Steinmeier.\par \par \uc2 Along with Russian stocks, which fell about 10%, the shares of Western companies like Renault, Danish brewer \uc2 Carlsberg A/S\uc2  and British oil giant \uc2 BP PLC\uc2  also fell on fears their big Russian investments would suffer as tensions over Ukraine grow.\par \par \uc2 "Russia is a very important market for us, much more than just for pure business considerations," said Johan de Nysschen, chief executive of Infiniti, the premium brand of Nissan, a Renault affiliate. He said that the company was monitoring the situation "very, very carefully."\par \par \uc2 Russia is \uc2 PepsiCo\uc2 's second-largest market by revenue after the U.S. The Purchase, N.Y.-based snack and beverage giant has billions of dollars of assets in the country. It declined to comment on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Entanglements between Russia and Europe go much further than with U.S. companies. Europe is a major buyer of Russian natural gas; Russia is a strong market for German goods; energy companies in Italy, the U.K. and elsewhere have major investments in Russia.\par \par \uc2 The ties make punishing Russia practically difficult because of the divisions within Europe between those close to Russia and those with less to lose.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Charles Grant\uc2 , director of the \uc2 Centre for European Reform\uc2 , a London-based think tank, warned against exaggerating Russia's economic leverage over Europe.\par \par \uc2 Cutting off gas supplies to Europe via Ukraine--a perennial Russian threat--would do plenty of damage to Russia too, he said. "We need (Russia), and it needs us."\par \par \uc2 Many companies selling goods into Russia are also facing the prospect of a suddenly weaker economy there.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply raised interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to stem the ruble's slide, helping the ruble to trim its losses from a post-Soviet low by the end of the day. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials pledged to spend as much as needed to defend the ruble.\par \par \uc2 "We're the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the fourth-largest reserves in the world and we can feel pretty comfortable about our ability to influence the situation," Kseniya Yudaeva, the bank's first deputy, told state television.\par \par \uc2 Economists agreed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could keep the ruble stable even with continued strong selling pressure. But higher rates could squeeze spending if they persist.\par \par \uc2 The market turmoil could also provide an economic boost for Russia in the form of higher prices for oil and gas, its main exports. Combined with a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that means Russia actually ends up with more rubles in its budget to spend to offset any discomfort from the economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 "They can spend more even without needing financing," said Jacob Nell, economist at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Economic sanctions that would hurt Russia without torpedoing the global economy are hard to find, he said. Russia's oil output is too big for other producers to make up, while shutting off natural gas exports would leave European countries scrambling for other sources. Import restrictions would also be unlikely to hurt much because Russia produces its own food and fuel.\par \par \uc2 Targeted visa and financial restrictions on top officials and their families, many of whom own property and other assets in the U.S. and Europe, have proved effective at getting Moscow's attention in the past.\par \par \uc2 "There is some real traction there, but it takes some time to work up," said Mr. Nell.\par \par \uc2 EU foreign ministers kept that option off the table Monday. But after the meeting, Mr. Fabius, the French foreign minister, told BFM TV that measures could eventually include an asset freeze targeting Russian officials. Asked if that could include Mr. Putin and his inner circle, Mr. Fabius said "that is not decided yet."\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 03, 2014 20:11 ET (01:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140304ea340002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
