\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Russian markets hit as Putin tightens grip on Crimea\b0\par\par\uc2 1,375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 13:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Moscow stocks fall 10 pct, rouble down 2.5 pct over war jitters\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine border guards say build-up of Russian armoured vehicles near Crimea\par \par \uc2 * EU ministers meet but no immediate sanctions expected\par \par \uc2 * Russian troops surround Ukrainian forces at two Crimea bases\par \par \uc2 * Demonstrators fly Russian flag in south, east Ukraine\par \par \uc2 By Lidia Kelly and Alissa de Carbonnel\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW/PEREVALNOYE, Ukraine, March 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  took a financial hit over its military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in neighbouring Ukraine, with its markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunging on Monday as President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's forces tightened their grip on the Russian-speaking Crimea region.\par \par \uc2 The Moscow stock market fell by 10 percent and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $10 billion of its reserves to prop up the rouble as investors took fright at escalating tensions with the West over the former Soviet republic.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine said Russia was building up armoured vehicles on its side of a narrow stretch of water closest to Crimea after Putin declared at the weekend he had the right to invade his neighbour to protect Russian interests and citizens.\par \par \uc2 On the ground in Perevalnoye, half way between the Crimean capital of Simferopol and the Black Sea, hundreds of Russian troops in trucks and armoured vehicles - without national insignia on their uniforms - surrounded two military compounds, confining Ukrainian soldiers as virtual prisoners.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine called up reservists on Sunday and the United States threatened to isolate Russia economically after Putin's action provoked what Britain's foreign minister called "the biggest crisis in Europe in the twenty-first century".\par \par \uc2 European Union\uc2  foreign ministers were to hold emergency talks on Ukraine on Monday but diplomats said they would not immediately match U.S. threats of sanctions against Moscow, but would focus on diplomatic efforts to prevent escalation.\par \par \uc2 The Organisation for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE) said it was trying to convene an international contact group to help defuse the crisis after Germany said Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  had convinced Putin to accept such an initiative.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland, which chairs the pan-European security body, said the contact group would support Ukraine during its transition and coordinate aid and could also discuss sending observers to monitor the rights of national minorities.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS TUMBLE, \uc2 GAZPROM\uc2  HIT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key lending rate by 1.5 percentage points after the rouble fell to all-time lows against the dollar. The MICEX index of Moscow stocks tumbled 10 percent to 1,294 points. Russian gas monopoly \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  , which supplies Europe through Ukraine, was down more than 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 Gazprom\uc2 's finance chief warned Ukraine that it may hike gas prices from next month, accusing Kiev of a patchy payments record, but said gas transit to Europe was normal.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's Prime Minister Arseny Yatseniuk, head of a pro-Western government that took power when former president Viktor Yanukovich, a Russian ally, fled on Feb. 21 after three months of street protests against his rule, said Putin had effectively declared war on his country.\par \par \uc2 Western leaders have reacted with a barrage of warnings to Putin against armed action, threatening economic and diplomatic consequences if Moscow goes further, but are not considering any military response.\par \par \uc2 A Ukrainian border guard spokesman said on Monday that Russian ships had been moving in and around the Crimean port city of Sevastopol, where the Russian Black Sea Fleet has a base, and that Russian forces had blocked mobile telephone services in some parts of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 He said Moscow was building up its armour near a ferry port on Russia's side of the 4.5 km (three mile) wide Kerch straight, which separates Crimea from Russia.\par \par \uc2 "There are armoured vehicles on the other side of the strait. We can't predict whether or not they will put any vehicles on the ferry," the spokesman said by telephone.\par \par \uc2 There was no immediate comment from the \uc2 Russian Defence Ministry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUSSIAN FLAGS FLYING\par \par \uc2 Russian forces have already bloodlessly seized Crimea - an isolated Black Sea peninsula with an ethnic Russian majority, where Moscow has a naval base.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday they surrounded several small Ukrainian military outposts there and demanded the Ukrainian troops disarm. Some refused, leading to stand-offs, although no shots were fired.\par \par \uc2 All eyes are now on whether Russia makes a military move in predominantly Russian-speaking eastern Ukraine, where pro-Moscow demonstrators have marched and raised Russian flags over public buildings in several cities in the last two days.\par \par \uc2 Russia has staged war games with 150,000 troops along the land border, but so far they have not crossed. Kiev says Moscow is orchestrating the protests to justify a wider invasion.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's security council ordered the general staff to immediately put all armed forces on highest alert. However, Kiev's small and underequipped military is seen as no match for Russia's superpower might.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Secretary of State John Kerry condemned Russia for what he called an "incredible act of aggression" and threatened "very serious repercussions".\par \par \uc2 G8 countries and other nations were prepared to "to go to the hilt to isolate Russia" if Moscow made the wrong choices in Ukraine, Kerry told CBS programme Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 "They are prepared to isolate Russia economically. The rouble is already going down. Russia has major economic challenges," he said. He mentioned visa bans, asset freezes and trade isolation as possible steps.\par \par \uc2 While the EU and NATO stepped up verbal pressure on Moscow, a German spokesman said Merkel believed it was not too late to resolve the Ukrainian crisis by political means despite differences of opinion between Putin and the West.\par \par \uc2 The German leader, who speaks fluent Russian, has had several long telephone calls with the German-speaking Putin since the crisis erupted with mass protests in Kiev.\par \par \uc2 "There is no doubt President Putin has a completely different view on the situation and events in Crimea from the \uc2 German government\uc2  and our Western partners," spokesman Steffen Seibert told reporters. But he added: "It is still not too late to resolve this crisis peacefully by political means."\par \par \uc2 U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon said he would ask Russia's foreign minister at a meeting in Geneva to refrain from acts or rhetoric that would further escalate the crisis. He was sending his deputy to convey the same message to the Ukrainian authorities in Kiev, he said.\par \par \uc2 So far, the Western response has been largely symbolic. Obama and others suspended preparations for a G8 summit in Sochi, where Putin has just finished staging his $50 billion winter Olympic games. Some countries recalled ambassadors. Britain said its ministers would stay away from the Paralympics due next in Sochi.\par \par \uc2 With the confrontation in Crimea having remained bloodless for days, a mood of imminent catastrophe has begun to ebb in Kiev, but many people are still on edge.\par \par \uc2 On Kiev's Independence Square, known as the Maidan, where protesters manned barricades for three months to bring down Yanukovich, the morning crowds were smaller than in the past few days as people returned to work.\par \par \uc2 "Crimea, we are with you!" read one placard. "Putin - Hitler of the 21st century," read another.\par \par \uc2 Sergei Lavreynenko, 44, a librarian from Kiev, said Ukrainians were ready to take up arms to defend the country, and were frustrated at mixed messages from the authorities.\par \par \uc2 "Of course we are all ready to go," he said next to a display of homemade mortar tubes and molotov cocktails used in the uprising against Yanukovich. "We have all served in the military. We have military specialisms. If we can build our own mortar tube like that, we can do even better.... But it needs to be organised. You can't just get a bunch of guys, grab sticks and clubs and race off to Crimea."\par \par \uc2 Britain's International Institute of Strategic Studies estimates Kiev has fewer than 130,000 troops under arms, with planes barely ready to fly and few spare parts for a single submarine.\par \par \uc2 Russia, by contrast, has spent billions under Putin to upgrade and modernise the capabilities of forces that were dilapidated after the breakup of the Soviet Union. Moscow's special units are now seen as equals of the best in the world. \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-3T13:02:18.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140303ea3300ftk\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Russian military  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  forces flight to safety\b0\par\par\uc2 CMC Markets   \par \uc2 299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 00:18\par \uc2 Fuseworks Media\par \uc2 FUSMED\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014. Fuseworks Limited.   \par \par \uc2 By Tracey Warren (Stockbroking BDM, CMC Markets Stockbroking)\par \par \uc2 Escalation in the Ukraine has sent investors scrambling, driving safe havens gold, crude and government debt higher. The flight to safety wiped nearly $60 billion off the Russian market with the index plunging nearly 11 percent. Stocks across Europe were sharply lower with the German DAX the hardest hit wiping 3.4 percent off the board. Wall Street wasn't immune with reverberations felt as the Dow and S&P both ended the session lower.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to support the Ruble spending 10bn as it spiralled lower. The beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to record lows against the dollar and Euro as the country's sovereign bonds also took a dive.\par \par \uc2 The scramble to safety drove the US Dollar and Yen higher overnight Russia's geopolitical tensions gripped markets. US data released overnight showing an increase in personal income and spending further supported the greenback. Despite the market turmoil the Australian dollar usually hard hit in times of volatility is still holding above US89c.\par \par \uc2 The escalating tensions between Moscow and the Ukraine saw gold and oil prices soar with gold futures up 2.2 percent at US $1350.3 an ounce, and Brent crude spiked to an intra-session high of US $112.39 per barrel. China data showing a slowdown in China factory activity saw industrial metals slump with copper prices at three month lows.\par \par \uc2 The local bourse today will be largely dictated by building approvals figures due at 11:30am and the RBA rate announcement at 2:30pm. A no change is expected as RBA Governor \uc2 Glenn Stevens\uc2  reiterated the need for a "period of stability in interest rates" in February's meeting.\par \par \uc2 Dokument FUSMED0020140303ea33006bw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
