\par\par \b \uc2 Consumers Turn Up the Heat, But Buy Little Else -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 15:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 09:05 EST - Consumer spending rises 0.4% in January. Impressive, right? Not so fast, says Ian Shepherdson of Pantheon Macroeconomics. "Excluding the 11.3% leap in real spending on utility electricity and gas -- cold weather boosted demand for heating energy -- real spending rose only 0.09%," he writes. In Shepherdson's view, sluggish income growth begets sluggish spending gains. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1400 GMT [Dow Jones] Most government bonds on offer at auctions from Austria, Germany, Spain and France this week are trading cheaply or at fair value, according to \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 . Austria's October 2018 is trading fair, while its October 2023 is trading cheap, says JPM ahead of Austria's EUR1.21 billion auction Tuesday. Both bonds have underperformed neighbors after the tap announcement on Feb. 24, although the underperformance of the October 2018 only corrected its expensiveness. As regards Germany's February 2019 Bobl, which is on offer on Wednesday, JPM says it's cheap, in a cheap part of the curve. Among the Spanish and French bonds on offer on Thursday, JPM reckons Spain's April 2024 and the October 2027 bond are "dear," while the others are, again, either fair or cheap. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 08:42 EST - Personal income comes in on expectations, rising 0.3% in January. That's a decent rate considering unemployment benefits dropped a large $15.5B annual rate as a program providing extended benefits expired in late December. On the other hand, Medicaid payments jumped at a $19.3B pace, related to coverage under the Affordable Care Act, says the Commerce Department. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1334 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/RUB slips back to 36.34 from the day's record high, close to 37.00. CitiFX's spot trader does not think its the CBR \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, rather that weak intraday longs are heading for the exit as the session moves towards the close, and the chance of a funding squeeze tomorrow. The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its policy interest rate by 150bp Monday taking it to 7%.gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1333 GMT [Dow Jones] Analysts at \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  do not expect any rate cuts from the ECB Thursday as the overall tone of the recent data releases are "mixed without providing much clarity on the macro picture." The bank is neutral on the short-end of the curve in the euro area, and over the medium term, continues to anticipate a step up in global activity, along with, "higher US rates to lift longer maturity German rates."(nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:27 EST - The broad flight into safer assets as tension between Ukraine and Russia intensifies has Treasury prices up to start the new month. The 10-year yield sunk as low as 2.59% overnight, its lowest in a month. The note is recently up 12/32 to yield 2.61%. Bond traders say there continues to be sellers around the 2.6% area, but mounting nerves about Ukraine could chip away at that resistance and send the yield toward 2.5%. Dollar is off 0.4% against the traditional safe-haven yen at Y101.35. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1325 GMT [Dow Jones] Expect more HUF weakness should EUR/HUF end the day above 313, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . The cross consolidated in February but fresh signs developed Monday that this corrective phase may end, it adds. A close Monday above 313.00 may trigger acceleration through 314.83, the February high, and then on to a test of critical resistance at 324.24, January 2012's all-time high, \uc2 UBS\uc2  strategist Richard Adcock says. First support lies at 308.45, last week's low, he adds. Recommends a new long on any close above 313.000, targeting 324.00. Stops will initially be placed at 308.00. EUR/HUF is at 312.31. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1318 GMT [Dow Jones] Russia is facing a serious risk of capital outflows, which will not be contained by Monday's emergency 150 basis point rate hike, says Benoit Anne at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . If the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  (CBR) is serious about financial stability it needs to send a much more powerful message, as an emergency rate hike is typically a tool reserved for CBs that can't afford direct \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On paper, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have that problem, sitting on roughly $490 billion of official reserves - and if the goal is to stabilize the RUB there's nothing better than credible direct \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . With reports Monday suggesting the CB sold about $10bln, Mr Anne says that for now at least this uninspiring policy response keeps him bearish on RUB, but at the same time he's remaining cautious of the CBR scaling up its policy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/RUB now at 36.45. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1242 GMT [Dow Jones] Geopolitical risks continued to weaken risk appetite globally and promote a cautious mood, also due to the loaded data agenda of the week, says \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi\uc2 . "Tighter TRY liquidity stance of CBRT and lower than expected January foreign trade deficit at $6.8 billion were not enough to lead to any meaningful outperformance, amidst ongoing political uncertainties," says TEB. It notes that the global data agenda, ECB's monthly meeting on Thursday and the US non-farm payrolls on Friday, will be important - but the market will also be monitoring developments in local political scene as well. USD/TRY now trades at 2.2236.(yeliz.candemir@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD drifting lower, Citi says there's plenty of chat doing the rounds of supply out of Russia as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebalances its reserves after this morning's heavy selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/RUB, with estimates in the USD10 billion area. EUR/USD now at 1.3761 from the day's 1.3793 high. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones] The Ukraine imbroglio has (unsurprisingly) spurred demand for haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Swiss franc clearly among them. However, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's 'floor' of CHF 1.20 to the euro still looks safe enough, even if it's closer than it was. The crisis would probably have to get a lot worse before markets felt like testing resolve in Zurich and Berne. The SNB has after all held the line at 1.20 since the end of 2011, when it stunned markets by imposing the floor. Investors have taken the hint since. The cross has chalked up its lowest point since January 10 2013, hitting CHF 1.21015, but it's back up at 1.2121 now. david.cottle@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1216 GMT [Dow Jones] While market expectations for ECB easing this week have retreated after the uptick in February's CPI estimate, economists at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  still look for policy easing this week. With the new ECB staff projections expected to show CPI falling well short of target through to 2016, the bank expects to see a 10 basis point cut in the refi rate, probably accompanied by a cut in the deposit rate into negative territory, and most likely and end to the sterilization of the ECB's EUR176 billion SMP portfolio. As for EUR/USD, the bank says Friday's post-CPI squeeze to a fresh 2014 high of 1.3824 likely reduced speculative positioning and if the ECB does deliver Thursday, the spot could move quickly lower. EUR/USD now at 1.3763. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 03, 2014 09:05 ET (14:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140303ea330020r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
