\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-\uc2 Sberbank\uc2  says no loans in Ukraine until situation improves\b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 19:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, detail)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, March 3 (Reuters) - Russian bank \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  will return to signing loan agreements in Ukraine when the financial situation there improves, the bank's chairman Igor Yushko said in a statement on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  previously said it had temporarily suspended some lending but the bank would continue to extend credit to large enterprises whose financial condition was sound.\par \par \uc2 "The bank continues to consider applications from potential borrowers building up a project portfolio. The bank will get back to signing loan agreements once financial situation improves," Yushko said.\par \par \uc2 He added that \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  had a strong liquidity level in Ukraine with 1.7 billion hryvnia available and an unused line of $750 million from its main bank.\par \par \uc2 It also said it was not reducing the size limits for cash withdrawals from payment cards.\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 's CEO German Gref said two weeks ago that the bank had no intention of leaving the market but had temporarily suspended some lending.\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  had exposure of 130 billion roubles ($4 billion) to Ukraine - or less than 1 percent of its balance sheet.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  took a financial hit over its military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in neighbouring Ukraine, with its markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunging on Monday as President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's forces tightened their grip on the Russian-speaking Crimea region.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week said it would limit the amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  citizens could take out to 15,000 hryvnia ($1,500) a day.\par \par \uc2 Some banks such as local bank Privatbank and the Ukrainian unit of Italy's biggest bank by assets, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , have announced limits. (Reporting by \uc2 Megan Davies\uc2 , editing by Gabriela Baczynska and Toby Chopra)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-6T18:33:16.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140303ea3300s5x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
