\par\par \b \uc2 Ukraine tensions spark global sell-off\b0\par\par\uc2 By John Balassi   \par \uc2 1,212 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 13:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 3 (IFR) - A global sell-off in risky assets is in full swing on this first business day of March, with the markets clearly focused on the Russian invasion of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 This instability will be a key market driver in the short term, and has given rise to further risk aversion this morning, with longer-dated Treasuries 4-6bp tighter and equity futures sharply down.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the geopolitical tension should do little to dampen the overall momentum in the US dollar-denominated high-grade market, which is fresh off the second-biggest February on record with over US$80bn in issuance.\par \par \uc2 Syndicate desks are looking for another active week, with supply forecasts in the US$25bn area for the week and between US$90bn and US$100bn for the whole of March. The employment report, the FOMC meeting on March 18-19, and a further escalation in the Eurasia region could certainly influence supply.\par \par \uc2 Several benchmark trades are expected to be front-loaded this week before the ECB policy meeting on Thursday and the non-farm payroll number on Friday. Solid technicals remain in place, and capital keeps surging into corporate bond funds; the investment-grade market has now seen inflows in 17 straight weeks, and a year-to-date total inflow of over US$18bn.\par \par \uc2 Aside from Friday's all-important employment report for February, the key releases this week will be today's ISM manufacturing PMI, the ADP private employment report and ISM services PMI data on Wednesday, and the weekly initial jobless claims and factory order figures on Thursday. Economists polled by Reuters are forecasting a rise in February's ISM manufacturing index to 52.0 from last month's 51.3 print.   Dow Futures: -151, S&P Futures: -20.25, Nasdaq Futures: -39.75 CDX IG21: 1.625bp wider, CDX HY21: 0.4375 point lower  10-yr UST yield: 2.611%, -4.9bp; 30-yr UST yield: 3.553%, -4.1bp\par \par \uc2 USD HIGH-GRADE PIPELINE Brazil's \uc2 Banco Daycoval SA\uc2 , rated Baa3/BB+/BBB-, has mandated \uc2 Bradesco\uc2  BBI, Ita\u250\'00\'FA BBA, J Safra Sarasin, and Standard Chartered to arrange a series of fixed income investor meetings in the US, Europe, and Asia commencing on March 6, 2014. A 144A/Reg S USD transaction may follow.\par \par \uc2 Hyundai Capital Services, Baa1/BBB+/BBB+, has mandated \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 , \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and \uc2 HSBC\uc2  to arrange meetings with fixed-income investors for a US dollar-denominated bond in the 144A/Reg S format.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \uc2 BBVA Bancomer\uc2  has mandated itself, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  to arrange meetings with fixed-income investors for a US dollar-denominated bond in the 144A/Reg S format.\par \par \uc2 OVERNIGHT REUTERS HEADLINES\par \par \uc2 RUSSIAN MARKETS HIT AS PUTIN TIGHTENS GRIP ON CRIMEA \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  took a financial hit over its military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in neighboring Ukraine, with its markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunging on Monday as President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's forces tightened their grip on the Russian-speaking Crimea region. The Moscow stock market fell by 10 percent and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $10 billion of its reserves to prop up the ruble as investors took fright at escalating tensions with the West over the former Soviet republic.\par \par \uc2 ASIAN FACTORY ACTIVITY STUMBLES Manufacturing activity among Asia's major export economies stumbled in February, led by China where data suggested growth in the factory sector was stalling. The \uc2 HSBC\uc2 /Markit purchasing managers index (PMI) on China manufacturing fell to 48.5 in February, a seven-month low and the third straight monthly decline in the index. A government PMI released on Saturday fell to 50.2, indicating the slowest growth in eight months.\par \par \uc2 EUROZONE FACTORY RECOVERY STUTTERS Growth in euro zone factory activity eased last month from January's 2-1/2 year high, but for the first time in almost three years output rose in all of the bloc's four biggest economies, a survey showed on Monday. Markit's final Eurozone Manufacturing Purchasing Managers' Index came in at 53.2 last month, up from a flash reading of 53.0 but below January's 54.0 - which was the highest since May 2011.\par \par \uc2 BUFFETT EYES BIG ACQUISITIONS AFTER RECORD PROFIT  \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  on Saturday signaled he plans to make more large acquisitions for \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway Inc\uc2  to expand his conglomerate, which posted a record profit in 2013 with the help of a recovering U.S. economy. Full-year profit rose 31 percent to $19.48 billion, or $11,850 per Class A share, while operating profit rose 20 percent to $15.14 billion, or $9,211 per share. For the fourth quarter, net profit rose nearly 10 percent to $4.99 billion, or $3,035 per share.\par \par \uc2 MEN'S WEARHOUSE\uc2  HAS MERGER AGREEMENT FROM JOS. A. BANK \uc2 Men's Wearhouse Inc\uc2  said it had entered into a non-disclosure agreement with Jos. A. Bank Clothiers Inc and received a draft merger agreement from the smaller company. The two men's apparel retailers have agreed to exchange certain confidential information and to work in good faith to evaluate a potential combination, \uc2 Men's Wearhouse\uc2  said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL MACRO - The Treasury belly leads bonds sharply higher amid risk off trade on Russia/Ukraine tensions - The tactical bias is neutral monitoring the fallout caused by the Ukrainian situation; look for a range of 2.64% to 2.56% in 10s - European risk markets hit on ascendant Ukraine tensions, core FI markets win out by default - Follows negative session for Asian stocks that were also unsettled by signs of mfg slowdown in China and North Korean missile test - Eurozone Feb final mfg PMI at 53.2 (flash 53.0) - Spain improves to 52.5 (prev 52.2, exp 52.0) but Italy disappoints at 52.3 (prev 53.1, exp 52.8)  - UK mortgage approvals hit fresh six year high in Jan at 76.947k (prev 72.798k rev, exp 73.5k) - UK Feb mfg PMI at 56.9 (prev 56.6 rev, exp 56.5) -employment index highest since May 2011 - China Feb mfg PMI data shows slowdown although better than feared  - Official China mfg PMI at released Sat at 50.2 (prev 50.5, exp 50.1) while \uc2 HSBC\uc2  mfg PMI at 48.5 (prev 48.3). Official svcs at 55.0 (prev 53.4) - European synthetic credit markets weaker, iTRAXX s-20 XOVER +11 at 269bp as at 11.11 GMT\par \par \uc2 US DATA 08:30 Personal Income (Jan) (mkt +0.2% m/m, prev 0.0% m/m) 08:30 Consumption (Jan) (mkt +0.1% m/m, prev +0.4% m/m) 08:30 Core PCE Prices (Jan) (mkt +0.1% m/m, prev +0.1% m/m) 08:58 Markit Mfg PMI (Feb) (prev 56.74) 10:00 Construction Spending (Jan) (mkt -0.5% m/m, prev +0.1% m/m) 10:00 ISM Mfg PMI (Feb) (mkt 52.0, prev 51.3)\par \par \uc2 EVENTS 11:00 Fed Outright Treasury Coupon Purchases (05/15/2021 - 02/15/2024) ($2.25 - $2.75 bln)   (Reporting by John Balassi; Additional reporting by the IFR team; Editing by Marc Carnegie)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-6T12:54:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140303ea3300hld\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
