\par\par \b \uc2 Up & Down Wall Street: "Hopeless, But Not Serious" -- Barron's\b0\par\par\uc2 1,645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 06:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 7\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM BARRON'S 3/3/14) \par    By Randall W. Forsyth \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Can it really be less than four years since markets got all in a tizzy because of violent demonstrations in Athens? Pictures of protestors setting fires were enough to touch off selling that led to the infamous Flash Crash of May 6, 2010. That afternoon, the Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbled 1,000 points, only to recoup most of those losses nearly as quickly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Since then, the markets have developed a sort of immunity to televised sights of mass demonstrations, even violent ones, after witnessing a seemingly unending parade of them. At the risk of sounding like Spiro Agnew, Richard Nixon's vice president until he resigned over corruption charges, when you've seen one political riot with burning cars, you've seen them all. They're all in some square with a bunch of kids recording the events for posterity on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The riots have played out in the Arab Spring, which ended up bringing neither democracy nor prosperity to the region. Syria's civil war continues with deaths well into six figures. After that, we're on overload when it comes to political upheavals abroad.\par \par \uc2 So, it shouldn't be too surprising that the U.S. stock market hit records last week against the backdrop of the crisis in Ukraine, which followed on the heels of protests in Venezuela and Thailand, and yet another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in Argentina.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Nigeria and Turkey -- the last two letters of the trendy MINT emerging markets, following Mexico and Indonesia -- saw their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure as the result of alleged high-level corruption. The head of the African nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspended recently for blowing the whistle on a massive oil fraud, allegedly involving top officials. Meanwhile, revelations that Turkey's prime minister, Recep Tayyip Edrogan, was caught on tape -- allegedly advising his son on how to get rid of millions of euros stashed in his home -- have renewed pressure on the lira, despite sharp interest-rate hikes in late January to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid the nation's ongoing economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Who can keep track of all these upheavals? And why should you? We Americans almost take pride in not giving a rodent's rectum about what happens beyond our shores. Especially if the Standard & Poor's 500 index notches a record amid our blissful ignorance. And just as we did in the late 1990s amid the civil war that split the former Yugoslavia, and resulted in the deaths of some 140,000 people.\par \par \uc2 The averages closed at a record on Friday, after retreating from their peaks amid reports of masked men taking over the two airports in Crimea, the pro-Russian southern region of Ukraine that has a major Russian naval base. The action was disavowed by Russian officials. After the close, President Obama warned \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  against military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 While much more is at stake politically for Vladimir Putin, the situation is "hopeless, but not serious," at least in economic terms, quips David P. Goldman, head of Americas strategy at Hong Kong-based Reorient. To put Ukraine's economy in perspective, he noted in a CNBC interview that its entire stock-market capitalization is roughly that of \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  (ticker: DIS), its gross domestic product is one-fifth of Turkey's, and its per capita income is a bit higher than Egypt's. Per capita income of Ukrainians is estimated at well less than half of Russians' and even less than that of Poles.\par \par \uc2 The smiley face put on all of these conflicts is that they only serve to make the U.S. stock market all the more attractive to investors. Ditto Europe, where those three immortal words uttered in mid-2012 by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Mario Draghi -- "whatever it takes" -- seem to have produced stability and confidence, if not exactly real prosperity.\par \par \uc2 That's best reflected in the plunge in yields of sovereign bonds of the erstwhile PIIGS -- Portugal, Italy, Ireland, Greece, and Spain. Ten-year yields are about 3.5% in Italy and Spain, and 3.1% in Ireland, while Portugal is under 5% and even Greece is under 7%. Lower borrowing costs for these countries effectively loosens a noose that previously had threatened to asphyxiate their economies. All of which is vastly more important than what happens to the former Soviet republics, provided, of course, that their conflicts don't escalate. But it's hard to envision blunders like those made in 1914 being repeated in 2014.\par \par \uc2 If U.S. investors look abroad, it should be in the other direction. In China last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fostered a sharp decline in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan or renminbi, including a steep 1.5% drop for the week ending Friday, the biggest downward movement since it stopped pegging it to the dollar in 2005.\par \par \uc2 At week's end, the renminbi traded at 6.1450 to the dollar, its lowest value in 10 months, and much weaker than its peak in January, when a buck bought only RMB6.0406.\par \par \uc2 That may not sound like much, but a move of "10 big figures," in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  parlance, to 6.14 to the buck from 6.04, is a big deal, especially given that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tightly controlled by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 . But, according to many observers, wagering on the continual appreciation of the renminbi had become too much of a one-way bet for Chinese officials.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, according to \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  economists, much of the capital outflow from other emerging markets -- some $100 billion from last May to December -- found its way to China, considered a haven, particularly after taking into account the dollar's 2% decline over that span. Subtracting $20 billion of fixed direct investment, the remaining $80 billion came from "hot-money" inflows.\par \par \uc2 JPM figures that much of that movement reflected greater use of the renminbi for the purposes of trades that previously had been settled in dollars, and borrowing in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On the latter point, if you were a Chinese business that could borrow in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that probably would decline, such as the Japanese yen, and use the proceeds to fund assets in appreciating renminbi, it's better than free money.\par \par \uc2 By some estimates, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  no longer is undervalued (although you'll get arguments on that point from many in the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 ). In response to lagging export growth and a weakening in its purchasing managers index, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  economists suggest that China could benefit from some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness.\par \par \uc2 "The risk," they continue, "is that PBoC actions worsen fears of a financial event in China." Signs of increased stress showed up in higher interbank interest rates, relative to risk-free interest rates.\par \par \uc2 In addition, Gavan Nolan, director of credit research at Markit, noted a sharp rise in the notional amount of credit-default swaps on China's sovereign credit, to $11.1 billion last week. "Leading indicators, such as the \uc2 HSBC\uc2 /Markit PMI, will continue to move markets, though the condition of China's shadow banking system is arguably more crucial," which has been noted here previously ("Risk-Off Redux," Jan. 27).\par \par \uc2 But the continued ascent of U.S. stocks, with the S&P 500 ending the week at another record and the Nasdaq Composite hitting a 13 1/2-year high, is inspiring a sense of deja vu for BCA Research managing editor Chen Zhao. "The broad economic and financial environment today is very similar to that of the second half of the 1990s, when the equity bull market turned parabolic," he writes.\par \par \uc2 He points to the shift in leadership to growth and cyclical sectors and away from dividend-paying and defensive stocks. "More interestingly," he says, "at the far end of the 'growth' spectrum are the 'social network' stocks, whose prices have been exploding upwards in recent months."\par \par \uc2 These high fliers trading at 12 to 13 times sales, while the Nasdaq Internet exchange-traded fund (PNQI) trades at 52 times earnings, which recalls the 1990's dot-com bubble. Zhao adds that analysts' assertions that social-network stocks are still cheap, based on their future profit potential, reminded him of the talk of that era.\par \par \uc2 Still, he adds, "There are increasing odds that the U.S. stock market will march to a much bubblier environment."\par \par \uc2 In particular, bull markets don't usually end until the yield curve inverts -- that is, short-term interest rates rise above longer-term bond yields. That's not likely to happen anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chair Janet Yellen reiterated in testimony to the Senate Banking Committee last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains on course to reduce its purchases of Treasury and agency mortgage-backed securities. But no hike in its key federal-funds rate target from the current level near zero is likely until well into 2015, even as the unemployment rate nears 6.5%. Yellen emphasized that the headline jobless rate didn't fully reflect the slack in the labor market.\par \par \uc2 This week should provide further clues to the economy's health, with the beginning of the slate of February indicators, especially the jobs report due out on Friday. The impact of the severe winter weather is uncertain, Yellen admitted, and economists in and out of government will be trying to weed out its effects.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the Fed will remain on its accommodative course, continuing to buoy asset prices. So, party like it's 1999, and don't worry what's happening in the rest of the world, except for China.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 e-mail: randall.forsyth@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 To subscribe to Barron's, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/subscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/subscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 01, 2014 00:12 ET (05:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140301ea31000mv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
