\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's freeze on grain restocking may be premature - SovEcon\b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2014\par 13:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Feb 14 (Reuters) - Russia's decision to freeze a programme to replenish state stocks next week may be premature if Kazakhstan takes advantage of its weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports to Russia, SovEcon agriculture analysts said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 One of the world's key wheat exporters, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has about 1.8 million tonnes of grain in "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks", which are used to boost domestic supply in years of poor harvests and to support prices in years of large crops.\par \par \uc2 The government planned to replenish the stocks during the 2013/14 marketing year, which started on July 1, but is far from its target as the prices it offers are below market rates.\par \par \uc2 "It's more lucrative for farmers to sell (grain) at the market price than to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks now," SovEcon said in a note. The government has bought 0.6 million tonnes of grain on the market in Siberia so far in 2013/14 out of plans to buy up to 6 million tonnes.\par \par \uc2 It will hold the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tender on Feb. 18 after which the programme will be frozen, the Agriculture ministry said on Friday. It bought 270 tonnes of grain - feed barley - for the stock on Feb. 12.\par \par \uc2 "This decision will be premature," SovEcon said.\par \par \uc2 Bigger imports from Kazakhstan may depress prices giving farmers in Siberia and Urals region reason to sell their grain to state stocks this spring, it added.\par \par \uc2 The maximum government price for the third class wheat has been 6,250 roubles ($180) per tonne on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tenders compared with an up to 6,600 roubles market price in Siberia last week.\par \par \uc2 Kazakhstan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalued the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 19 percent on Feb. 11 to about 185 per dollar to prevent large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation and adjust to a freer rouble float in Russia, Kazakhstan's main trading partner.\par \par \uc2 Central Asia's largest grain producer, Kazakhstan still has up to 4.5 million tonnes of grains available for export until the end of 2013/14 marketing year, according to official data.\par \par \uc2 Russian Agriculture ministry was not able to comment immediately on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tenders would be resumed later this year.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 35.2125 Russian roubles) (Reporting by Polina Devitt, editing by David Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-14T13:52:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140214ea2e008ob\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
