\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar, stocks advance after strong U.S. GDP\b0\par\par\uc2 880 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2014\par 23:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Emerging markets turmoil eases, Russia pledges on ruble\par \par \uc2 * Gold, U.S. Treasuries fall after U.S. GDP report\par \par \uc2 * U.S., European stocks rebound\par \par \uc2 * Emerging markets equities pare losses\par \par \uc2 By Herbert Lash\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 30 (Reuters) - The dollar strengthened and global equity markets rebounded on Thursday after data showed the U.S. economy grew strongly in the last quarter of 2013, a rate that suggests solid corporate earnings and continued solid growth into the new year.\par \par \uc2 Turmoil in emerging markets eased, as the hard-hit Turkish lira and South African rand rebounded. \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledged unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the ruble strays outside its target band.\par \par \uc2 "There was a general rebound in risk sentiment," said Jeffrey Young, U.S. interest rate strategist at \uc2 Nomura Securities International\uc2  in New York. "Some confidence grew back with the emerging markets story no longer developing."\par \par \uc2 The ruble hit record lows against the euro early in the session, while the rand slid to multi-year troughs before rebounding. Moves earlier in the week by Turkey, South Africa and India to staunch capital flight had failed.\par \par \uc2 Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have felt the heat since the U.S. Federal Reserve said it would begin to trim its stimulus, a program that has injected more than $3 trillion into the U.S. economy and world markets since the onset of the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Commerce Department reported that gross domestic product grew at a 3.2 percent annual rate in the fourth quarter, which combined with the 4.1 percent pace in the third quarter resulted in the biggest half-year increase since 2003.\par \par \uc2 The GDP growth, which was in line with economists' expectations, drove a turnaround in equity markets in Europe and a broad rise in Wall Street stocks. The S&P 500 scored its biggest gain in more than a month, and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  composite index gained more than 2 percent at one point, helped by strong quarterly revenue growth at \uc2 Facebook Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  shares rose 14.1 percent to finish at a record closing high of $61.08.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.7 percent against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at 81.069, and the appeal of the greenback revived against the safe-haven yen and Swiss franc.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of concern over what's happening overseas but in the domestic (U.S.) market GDP was pretty strong," said Cam Albright, director of asset allocation at \uc2 Wilmington Trust Investment Advisors\uc2  in Wilmington, Delaware. "If you see growth in the economy pointing to higher earnings growth, that will give markets more confidence in the United States."\par \par \uc2 The Fed, as expected, said on Wednesday it will cut its monthly bond purchases by another $10 billion in February, to $65 billion, as policymakers see less need for stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "The end of QE is an indication the economy is getting better, though I'm not sure the market has entirely transitioned to that idea yet," Albright said.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell 0.81 percent against the dollar at $1.3552, while against the yen the greenback was up 0.38 percent at 102.67 yen.\par \par \uc2 A measure of global equity markets, MSCI's all-country world index, rebounded, rising 0.19 percent. MSCI's emerging markets index, however, was slightly lower, down 0.22 percent.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the Dow Jones industrial average rose 109.82 points, or 0.7 percent, to 15,848.61. The S&P 500 gained 19.99 points, or 1.13 percent, to 1,794.19, and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Composite added 71.691 points, or 1.77 percent, to 4,123.125.\par \par \uc2 In after-hours trade, shares of \uc2 Google Inc\uc2  jumped 4.4 percent to $1,186, after closing up 2.6 percent in regular \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  trade. \uc2 Google\uc2  reported quarterly revenue that beat Wall Street targets after the market close.\par \par \uc2 In Europe the pan-regional FTSEurofirst 300 index of leading companies closed up 0.3 percent at 1,294.26.\par \par \uc2 Gold fell more than 2 percent, on track for its biggest one-day drop in more than a month, as the U.S. GDP data and the Fed's decision to keep trimming its stimulus boosted the dollar and led traders to cash in gains in the metal.\par \par \uc2 U.S. COMEX gold futures for February delivery settled down $20 an ounce at $1,242.20.\par \par \uc2 Brent oil rose to just over $108 per barrel, and U.S. crude reached its highest level in a month on bitter cold weather in the United States, which has boosted heating oil demand.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude rose 10 cents to settle at $107.95 a barrel. U.S. crude settled up 87 cents at $98.23.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. bond market retreated following Wednesday's rally, but some of the money leaving equities found its way into the developed world's government bond market.\par \par \uc2 German Bund futures rose 33 ticks to settle at 143.23, while German 10-year yields fell to their lowest level in nearly six months before ending at 1.712 percent.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark 10-year Treasury notes were last down 5/32 in price to yield 2.6949 percent.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-2T22:07:08.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140130ea1u00huf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
