\par\par \b \uc2 Why Can Ruble Continue To Fall Even As  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  Successfully  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2 ? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2014\par 17:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ruble has come under fire in this year's emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sell-off. Year-to-date, ruble lost about 7% against the dollar and, earlier this week, dropped to its lowest value against the dollar since March, 2009.\par \par \uc2 Ruble is not a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  associated with current account deficits, so why has there been a sell-off? Michael Shaoul at Marketfield Asset Management explains:\par \par \uc2 What is important about the RUB is that it is not a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  associated with current account deficits and has also been largely out of the headlines in recent weeks. On the other hand Russia does share very close trade and financial ties with Turkey (much of which takes place in the gray economy) and it is therefore unsurprising that the collapse of the TRY has spilled across the Black Sea to its larger neighbor. We would advise readers to watch the RUB and local equity and bond markets closely in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 So far, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the ruble. Given the size of Russia's foreign reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has plenty of room to maneuver. Here is Capital Economics:\par \par \uc2 For now, the scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been relatively small. The Bank has so far sold $5bn this month (to give some perspective, during the 2008/09 ruble devaluation the CBR was selling over $40bn a month). With some $500bn in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, it can stomach \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on this scale for some time.\par \par \uc2 But it would do us well to understand how \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  operates. It is possible that the ruble will keep on falling even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  successfully \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because the exchange rate corridor that \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  operates on is not fixed but a moving target. Here is Capital Economics again:\par \par \uc2 The CBR allows the ruble to float freely against its dollar-euro basket (0.55*US$+0.45*Euro) when it is within a "neutral" corridor, currently 35.9-38.9/basket. If the exchange rate is up to one ruble outside this, the CBR \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but this is limited to $200mn per day (and limited to $400mn per day if the exchange rate is between one and two rubles outside the neutral corridor). If the exchange rate is more than two rubles higher or lower than the neutral corridor, then the CBR will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in unlimited quantities.\par \par \uc2 however, for every $350mn of the CBR's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it moves the corridor by five kopeks towards the current exchange rate. As such, even if the CBR is selling unlimited quantities of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it isn't defending a specific peg. In other words, as the CBR steps up \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble can continue to weaken\par \par \uc2 Year-to-date, the \uc2 Market Vectors Russia ETF Trust\uc2  (RSX) lost 12.5%, the iShares MSCI Turkey ETF (TUR) slumped 13.6%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 30, 2014 11:58 ET (16:58 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140130ea1u002ts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
