\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging-Market  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Claw Back Some Ground -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tommy Stubbington   \par \uc2 1,488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2014\par 16:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered from earlier losses on Thursday, as U.S. markets opened to some upbeat economic data and corporate earnings.\par \par \uc2 But broad risk sentiment recovered later in the session, after data confirmed the U.S. economy grew 3.2% in the fourth quarter of 2013. U.S. stocks opened higher, recovering some of Wednesday's losses, while European indexes bounced back from early declines.\par \par \uc2 The recent pullback from emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had continued earlier Thursday, after the U.S. Federal Reserve confirmed expectations that it would scale back its bond-buying program by a further $10 billion a month.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian forint bore the brunt of the early selling pressure, mimicking large declines in the Turkish lira and South African rand earlier in the week--drops that surprise rate rises from central banks have been powerless to reverse in the short term.\par \par \uc2 But the forint recovered its early losses against the euro, as some analysts said the broad weakness in emerging markets looked exaggerated.\par \par \uc2 "The market appears to be treating tapering as actual tightening, which is an overreaction. Tactically, emerging markets look oversold," said George Iwanicki, global emerging markets strategist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "As painful as it may be in the short term, it is actually very positive that the brunt of the pain from tapering is being felt through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustments; this is making emerging markets more competitive as a whole. Encouragingly, we are seeing central banks responding with orthodox moves like rate hikes."\par \par \uc2 Turkey and South Africa--among a group of emerging markets that have struggled to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors adjust to more restrictive monetary policy in big economies--also benefited from the more optimistic tone.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira bounced from earlier lows to strengthen 0.8% to 224.34 lira to the dollar. Despite the recovery, it remains at a similar level to where it traded before the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised rates.\par \par \uc2 The South African rand added 1.6% against the buck, having earlier traded close to a five-year low.\par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble, which hit a near-five-year low earlier Thursday, clawed back 0.5% against the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was given an added boost after the head of \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there were no plans to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 2014.\par \par \uc2 Hungary and \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  have moved into the market's cross hairs as the recent spate of emerging-market rate increases-- India also bumped up rates earlier this week--ramped up the pressure on countries that haven't followed suit.\par \par \uc2 "When Turkey got bullied into hiking rates by the market, there were two next: South Africa and Russia, neither of which had hiked and had become low yielders. The Hungarian forint is next," said Bartosz Pawlowski, global head of emerging-market strategy at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has room to continue cutting interest rates, its governor said Wednesday, despite the recent weakness in the forint. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trimming rates in small increments for several months.\par \par \uc2 But Hungary's large reserves of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  give it greater room for a fightback than many emerging-market nations.\par \par \uc2 "If officials get uncomfortable with the slide in the forint, I think they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They have the artillery to do that," Mr. Pawlowski said.\par \par \uc2 European equities picked up after the U.S. data, having earlier been dragged down by the tension surrounding emerging markets. The Stoxx Europe 600 index was 0.3% higher, while Germany's DAX added 0.4%. London's FTSE 100 lagged behind, paring losses to trade at 0.1% down.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., the S&P 500 was up 0.7% in early trade.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, the Nikkei had dived 2.5%, wiping out most of Wednesday's gains, which came before the positive effects of the Turkish rate rise melted away.\par \par \uc2 In a further knock to global confidence, purchasing managers' data for January brought more evidence that manufacturing in China is slowing.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the New Zealand dollar remained lower after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates on hold. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  reported widespread selling of the New Zealand dollar against its Australian counterpart, with funds, companies and banks all joining the fray.\par \par \uc2 Brad Frischkorn contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tommy Stubbington at tommy.stubbington@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 30 Jan 2014 11:56 EDT  Emerging-Market \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Claw Back Some Ground -- Update\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Tommy Stubbington \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recovered from earlier losses Thursday, as U.S. mark