\par\par \b \uc2 Russia could consider delaying rouble float -econ minister\b0\par\par\uc2 471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2014\par 20:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Jan 28 (Reuters) - Russia could delay plans to move to a floating rouble and instead consider limits on the exchange rate, Economy Minister Alexei Ulyukayev said on Tuesday after a sharp sell-off in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Under the current conditions, there are other possible approaches to the concept of a floating rouble and to the pace of their implementation," Ulyukayev told journalists at a briefing.\par \par \uc2 "\u207\'00\'CF would have discussed the possibility of introducing certain limits on the degree of freedom of the exchange rate depending on several factors related to financial stability."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has announced plans to cease targeted \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and shift to a free float of the rouble by 2015, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to absorb the shocks of the country's weakening economy.\par \par \uc2 "\u207\'00\'CF might have discussed timing and maybe moving it," Ulyukayev said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to target inflation rather than the exchange rate has been seen as potentially helping the economy, but it has also exacerbated a weakening in the rouble.\par \par \uc2 Worries about China's economy and expectations of the United States easing its monetary stimulus have pushed the rouble to all-time lows against the euro and 4.5 percent down versus the dollar amid a rout in emerging markets, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke its fall on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev said he sees inflation in 2014 below 5 percent. The annual increase in consumer prices stood at 6.5 percent in December, above the bank's 5-6 percent target range for this year.\par \par \uc2 "The risk for economic growth is more salient and acute than the risk of inflation ... Formally there is no stagflation. There is a risk (of it) but I believe that we have a pretty clear perspective on inflation. It will decrease and the risk to growth is much greater."\par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev also said that state companies should pay only 25 percent of their profits in dividends, rather than a 35 percent figure proposed by the Finance Ministry under International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS).\par \par \uc2 In the long-running debate, Russia has been considering whether to make state companies pay higher dividends to replenish government coffers and reassure foreign investors worried by a slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 The government told state companies in November 2012 that they must pay at least 25 percent of their income in dividends, an increase from 15 percent. A later proposal pushed it to 35 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We do not think it relevant for state companies to raise dividends to 35 percent in the medium term. We are no longer discussing 35 percent, 25 percent IFRS is reasonable," Ulyukayev said. He said it was important to have a clear dividend policy and move gradually to 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-1-31T19:14:00.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140128ea1s00ei9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
