\par\par \b \uc2 Russia Economy Minister Says Slow Growth Is Higher Risk\b0\par\par\uc2 367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2014\par 19:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--Russia faces more risks of slow growth than of high inflation, even despite a sharp weakening of the ruble, the country's economy minister said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters, Alexei Ulyukayev said, "Risks for the growth are more acute and present, than the risks of high inflation," adding that Russia isn't in stagflation--a combination of slow growth and high inflation.\par \par \uc2 Russia's deputy head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ksenia Yudayeva said Jan. 15 that Russia is facing stagflation. Russia's economic growth slowed to 1.4% in 2013, a far cry from an average of about 7% before the financial crisis of 2008, or 5% a year set by President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as a midterm goal. Inflation stood at 6.5% in 2013 above the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target.\par \par \uc2 However Mr. Ulyukayev was adamant Russia's inflation will stay below 5% in 2014 even amid the ruble's weakening. The ruble is trading at its 5-year's low against the dollar, off more than 3% since the start of the year after shedding 10% in 2013.\par \par \uc2 The bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is gradually withdrawing from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , setting 2015 as a target for a fully free-floating ruble and inflation-targeting policy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ulyukayev, who until mid-2013 was the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first deputy, said he'd discuss a possibility of postponing the introduction of these measures and a broader approach no a nonintervention policy.\par \par \uc2 "I'd probably put to a discussion a possibility to introduce some limitations to the free floating regime depending on financial stability, and, possibly discuss the timing of the introduction [of the regime]," he said.\par \par \uc2 The minister sees investment as the key to regaining the economy's momentum. To this end he supports consolidation in the banking sector, stronger loan guaranties and measures to decrease lenders' liabilities, notably their deposit rates, to permit lenders' credit business and households at lower rates.\par \par \uc2 The minister added "it's highly probable Russia's current account will remain positive, albeit declining in midterm."\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 28-01-14 1856GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140128ea1s000bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
